§§ `Is my case for the Singapore International Commercial Court? (ss 18D to 18M)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 18D(1). The Singapore International Commercial Court has jurisdiction to hear and try an action that satisfies ALL of: it is international and commercial in nature; the General Division may hear and try it in its original civil jurisdiction; and the other conditions the Rules prescribe.
@export Under section 18D(1), does the Singapore International Commercial Court have jurisdiction to hear and try my action?
GIVEN `my action is international and commercial in nature` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your action international and commercial in nature?
      `the General Division may hear and try my action in its original civil jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it an action the General Division may hear and try in its original civil jurisdiction?
      `my action satisfies the other conditions the Rules of Court prescribe` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your action satisfy the other conditions the Rules of Court prescribe for the Singapore International Commercial Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 18D(1), does the Singapore International Commercial Court have jurisdiction over my action` IS
    `s 18D(1) the Singapore International Commercial Court has jurisdiction to hear and try the action` `my action is international and commercial in nature` `the General Division may hear and try my action in its original civil jurisdiction` `my action satisfies the other conditions the Rules of Court prescribe`

-- s 18D(3)(b). Where the Court has and exercises jurisdiction over an action, that includes a counterclaim brought in and remaining part of it, if the action and counterclaim taken as a whole maintain an international and commercial character.
@export Under section 18D(3)(b), does the Singapore International Commercial Court's jurisdiction include my counterclaim?
GIVEN `the Singapore International Commercial Court has and exercises jurisdiction over the action under section 18D(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Singapore International Commercial Court have, and exercise, jurisdiction over the action under section 18D(1)?
      `my counterclaim was brought in, and remains part of, that action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your counterclaim brought in, and does it remain part of, the action?
      `the action and counterclaim taken as a whole maintain an international and commercial character` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Taken as a whole, do the action and the counterclaim maintain an international and commercial character?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 18D(3)(b), does the Court's jurisdiction include my counterclaim` IS
    `s 18D(3)(b) the jurisdiction includes hearing and trying the counterclaim` `the Singapore International Commercial Court has and exercises jurisdiction over the action under section 18D(1)` `my counterclaim was brought in, and remains part of, that action` `the action and counterclaim taken as a whole maintain an international and commercial character`

-- s 18F(1), (2). Parties to an agreement to submit to the Singapore International Commercial Court's jurisdiction are considered to have agreed to the three terms, unless the agreement expressly provides otherwise.
@export Under section 18F(1), are we considered to have agreed to this term of our agreement to submit to the Singapore International Commercial Court?
GIVEN `the term` IS A `s 18F(1) agreed term` @desc Which term: (a) submitting to the exclusive jurisdiction of the Singapore International Commercial Court; (b) carrying out its judgments and orders without undue delay; or (c) waiving recourse to any court or tribunal outside Singapore?
      `we have an agreement to submit to the jurisdiction of the Singapore International Commercial Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there an agreement between the parties to submit to the jurisdiction of the Singapore International Commercial Court?
      `our agreement has an express provision to the contrary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the agreement have an express provision to the contrary?
      `the recourse can be validly waived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For waiver of recourse outside Singapore: can the recourse be validly waived?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 18F(1), are we considered to have agreed to the term` IS
    `s 18F(1) the parties are considered to have agreed to the term` `the term` `we have an agreement to submit to the jurisdiction of the Singapore International Commercial Court` `our agreement has an express provision to the contrary` `the recourse can be validly waived`

-- s 18J(1). The Singapore International Commercial Court may transfer a case commenced in that Court to the General Division, in accordance with the Rules of Court.
@export Under section 18J(1), may the Singapore International Commercial Court transfer my case to the General Division?
GIVEN `my case was commenced in the Singapore International Commercial Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your case commenced in the Singapore International Commercial Court?
      `the transfer is in accordance with the Rules of Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the transfer in accordance with the Rules of Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 18J(1), may the Singapore International Commercial Court transfer my case to the General Division` IS
    `s 18J(1) the Singapore International Commercial Court may transfer the case to the General Division` `my case was commenced in the Singapore International Commercial Court` `the transfer is in accordance with the Rules of Court`

-- s 18J(2). The General Division may transfer a case commenced in it to the Singapore International Commercial Court, in accordance with the Rules of Court.
@export Under section 18J(2), may the General Division transfer my case to the Singapore International Commercial Court?
GIVEN `my case was commenced in the General Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your case commenced in the General Division?
      `the transfer is in accordance with the Rules of Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the transfer in accordance with the Rules of Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 18J(2), may the General Division transfer my case to the Singapore International Commercial Court` IS
    `s 18J(2) the General Division may transfer the case to the Singapore International Commercial Court` `my case was commenced in the General Division` `the transfer is in accordance with the Rules of Court`

-- s 18M. A party to a case in the Singapore International Commercial Court, or to an appeal from it, may in accordance with the Rules of Court be represented by a foreign lawyer or law expert registered under Part 4B of the Legal Profession Act 1966.
@export Under section 18M, may I be represented by a registered foreign lawyer or law expert in the Singapore International Commercial Court?
GIVEN `I am a party to a case in the Singapore International Commercial Court or to an appeal from that Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a party to a case in the Singapore International Commercial Court, or to an appeal from that Court?
      `my representative is a foreign lawyer or law expert registered under Part 4B of the Legal Profession Act 1966` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person a foreign lawyer or law expert registered in accordance with Part 4B of the Legal Profession Act 1966?
      `the representation is in accordance with the Rules of Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the representation in accordance with the Rules of Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 18M, may I be represented by a registered foreign lawyer or law expert` IS
    `s 18M the party may be represented by the foreign lawyer or law expert` `I am a party to a case in the Singapore International Commercial Court or to an appeal from that Court` `my representative is a foreign lawyer or law expert registered under Part 4B of the Legal Profession Act 1966` `the representation is in accordance with the Rules of Court`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Can I appeal to the High Court, and do I need permission? (ss 20, 21, 22, 22A, 22B)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 20. The appellate civil jurisdiction of the General Division consists of appeals from Family Courts, and from District Courts and Magistrates' Courts, exercising jurisdiction of a quasi-criminal or civil nature, and from other tribunals as a written law prescribes.
@export Under section 20, is my appeal part of the appellate civil jurisdiction of the General Division?
GIVEN `the court or tribunal I am appealing from` IS A Court @desc Which court or tribunal made the decision you are appealing from?
      `the nature of the case` IS A `Nature of the proceeding` @desc Is the case civil, quasi-criminal or criminal?
      `a written law prescribes an appeal from this tribunal to the General Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it is a tribunal other than a court named in the Act: does a written law prescribe an appeal from it to the General Division?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 20, is my appeal part of the General Division's appellate civil jurisdiction` IS
    `s 20 the appellate civil jurisdiction of the General Division consists of hearing the appeal` `the court or tribunal I am appealing from` `the nature of the case` `a written law prescribes an appeal from this tribunal to the General Division`

-- s 21(1). Subject to the Act and any other written law, an appeal lies to the General Division from a decision of a District Court or Magistrate's Court ONLY with permission of that court or the General Division, in these cases:
-- (a) the amount in dispute or the value of the subject matter (excluding interest and costs) does not exceed $60,000 (or the amount an order under s 21(3) specifies); (b) a case specified in the Third Schedule.
@export Under section 21(1), can I appeal to the General Division from this District Court or Magistrate's Court decision?
GIVEN `the court that made the decision` IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision you want to appeal: a District Court, a Magistrate's Court, or another?
      `the amount in dispute, in dollars, or 0 if I do not know` IS A NUMBER @desc What was the amount in dispute, or the value of the subject matter, at the hearing before that court, excluding interest and costs, in dollars? (0 if you do not know)
      `an order has changed the $60,000 figure to this amount, in dollars, or 0 if none` IS A NUMBER @desc If the President has by order varied the $60,000 figure, what is the new amount in dollars? (0 if no order has varied it)
      `the Third Schedule covers my case` IS A `scja1 seat answer` @desc Is your case one specified in the Third Schedule?
      `the court that made the decision has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the District Court or Magistrate's Court that made the decision given you permission to appeal?
      `the General Division has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the General Division given you permission to appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 21(1), can I appeal to the General Division from the decision` IS
    `s 21(1) an appeal lies to the General Division from the decision`
        (`scja1 seat my decision from what I know` `the court that made the decision` `appellate jurisdiction` FALSE `the amount in dispute, in dollars, or 0 if I do not know` `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` FALSE `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` FALSE)
        (`scja1 seat my amount if I know it` `an order has changed the $60,000 figure to this amount, in dollars, or 0 if none`)
        (`scja1 seat what I say, as a fact` `the Third Schedule covers my case`)
        `the court that made the decision has given permission` `the General Division has given permission`

-- s 21(1), the question that matters first: do I need permission? JUST TRUE if the case is within (a) or (b); the Act does not say that permission is NOT needed in any other case.
@export Under section 21(1), do I need permission to appeal to the General Division from this District Court or Magistrate's Court decision?
@desc The Act says an appeal needs permission in two cases: where the amount in dispute or value of the subject matter does not exceed $60,000 (or the amount an order sets), and in a case in the Third Schedule. JUST TRUE means you are in one of them. NOT COVERED does not mean you need no permission: this section does not say that you need permission in any other case (for example a claim of $75,000), so look at the Schedules and any other provision.
GIVEN `the court that made the decision` IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision you want to appeal: a District Court, a Magistrate's Court, or another?
      `the amount in dispute, in dollars, or 0 if I do not know` IS A NUMBER @desc What was the amount in dispute, or the value of the subject matter, at the hearing before that court, excluding interest and costs, in dollars? (0 if you do not know)
      `an order has changed the $60,000 figure to this amount, in dollars, or 0 if none` IS A NUMBER @desc If the President has by order varied the $60,000 figure, what is the new amount in dollars? (0 if no order has varied it)
      `the Third Schedule covers my case` IS A `scja1 seat answer` @desc Is your case one specified in the Third Schedule?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 21(1), do I need permission to appeal from the decision` IS
    IF `the court that made the decision` EQUALS `District Court` OR `the court that made the decision` EQUALS `Magistrate's Court`
    THEN `s 21(1) permission is needed to appeal from the decision`
            (`scja1 seat my decision from what I know` `the court that made the decision` `appellate jurisdiction` FALSE `the amount in dispute, in dollars, or 0 if I do not know` `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` FALSE `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` FALSE)
            (`scja1 seat my amount if I know it` `an order has changed the $60,000 figure to this amount, in dollars, or 0 if none`)
            (`scja1 seat what I say, as a fact` `the Third Schedule covers my case`)
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a decision of a District Court or Magistrate's Court"

-- s 21(2A). An order of the General Division giving or refusing permission under section 21(1) is final.
@export Under section 21(2A), is the General Division's order giving or refusing permission to appeal final?
GIVEN `it is an order of the General Division giving or refusing permission under section 21(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it an order of the General Division giving or refusing permission to appeal under section 21(1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 21(2A), is the order final` IS
    `s 21(2A) the order is final` `it is an order of the General Division giving or refusing permission under section 21(1)`

-- s 22(1). All appeals to the General Division in its appellate civil jurisdiction are by way of rehearing.
@export Under section 22(1), is my appeal to the General Division by way of rehearing?
GIVEN `my appeal is to the General Division in the exercise of its appellate civil jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your appeal an appeal to the General Division in the exercise of its appellate civil jurisdiction?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 22(1), is my appeal by way of rehearing` IS
    `s 22(1) the appeal is by way of rehearing` `my appeal is to the General Division in the exercise of its appellate civil jurisdiction`

-- s 22(4), (5). Further evidence may be given to the General Division only with its permission and on special grounds, except that evidence relating to matters occurring after the date of the decision appealed against may be given without permission.
@export Under section 22(4), may I give further evidence on my appeal to the General Division?
GIVEN `the date the matters the evidence relates to occurred` IS A DATE @desc On what date did the matters the further evidence relates to occur?
      `the date of the decision I am appealing against` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the decision you are appealing against made?
      `the General Division has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the General Division given permission for the further evidence to be given?
      `there are special grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are there special grounds for giving the further evidence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 22(4), may I give further evidence on my appeal` IS
    `s 22(4) the further evidence may be given to the General Division` `the date the matters the evidence relates to occurred` `the date of the decision I am appealing against` `the General Division has given permission` `there are special grounds`

-- s 22A(1). The General Division may decide any matter in its appellate civil jurisdiction without hearing oral arguments, other than a matter prescribed by the Rules of Court or the Family Justice Rules.
@export Under section 22A(1), may the General Division decide my appeal without hearing oral arguments?
GIVEN `my matter is in the appellate civil jurisdiction of the General Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter within the General Division's appellate civil jurisdiction?
      `the matter is prescribed by the Rules of Court or the Family Justice Rules` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the matter one that the Rules of Court or the Family Justice Rules prescribe as needing oral arguments?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 22A(1), may the General Division decide my appeal without hearing oral arguments` IS
    `s 22A(1) the General Division may decide the matter without hearing oral arguments` `my matter is in the appellate civil jurisdiction of the General Division` `the matter is prescribed by the Rules of Court or the Family Justice Rules`

-- s 22A(4). The General Division must not hear a matter asynchronously if that would be inconsistent with its duty to ensure the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties.
@export Under section 22A(4), must the General Division not hear my appeal by exchange of written correspondence?
GIVEN `to do so would be inconsistent with the court's duty to ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would hearing the matter by exchange of written correspondence be inconsistent with the court's duty to ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 22A(4), must the General Division not hear my appeal asynchronously` IS
    `s 22A(4) the General Division must not hear the matter in an asynchronous manner` `to do so would be inconsistent with the court's duty to ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties`

-- s 22B(1). The General Division may, on its own motion, summarily dismiss an appeal in its appellate civil jurisdiction, if it is satisfied that it lacks jurisdiction, that every issue was already decided in an earlier matter in which the appellant was involved, or that the conditions the Rules prescribe are met.
@export Under section 22B(1), may the General Division on its own motion summarily dismiss my appeal?
GIVEN `my appeal is to the General Division in the exercise of its appellate civil jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your appeal an appeal to the General Division in the exercise of its appellate civil jurisdiction?
      `the General Division is satisfied it does not have the jurisdiction to hear and determine the appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the General Division satisfied that it does not have the jurisdiction to hear and determine the appeal?
      `every issue in the appeal has already been decided in an earlier matter in which I was involved, so the appeal has no merit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the General Division satisfied that every issue in the appeal has already been decided by the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal in an earlier matter in which the appellant was involved, and that the appeal therefore has no merit?
      `the conditions the Rules prescribe are met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the General Division satisfied that the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court or the Family Justice Rules are met?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 22B(1), may the General Division summarily dismiss my appeal` IS
    `s 22B(1) the General Division may on its own motion summarily dismiss the appeal` `my appeal is to the General Division in the exercise of its appellate civil jurisdiction` `the General Division is satisfied it does not have the jurisdiction to hear and determine the appeal` `every issue in the appeal has already been decided in an earlier matter in which I was involved, so the appeal has no merit` `the conditions the Rules prescribe are met`

-- s 22B(2). Before summarily dismissing an appeal, the General Division must give the appellant a reasonable opportunity to show cause and consider the appellant's representations.
@export Under section 22B(2), can the General Division summarily dismiss my appeal now?
GIVEN `I was given a reasonable opportunity to show cause why the appeal should not be summarily dismissed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you given a reasonable opportunity to show cause why your appeal should not be summarily dismissed?
      `the General Division considered my representations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the General Division consider your representations?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 22B(2), can the General Division summarily dismiss my appeal now` IS
    `s 22B(2) the General Division may summarily dismiss the appeal under section 22B(1) now, as the section requires` `I was given a reasonable opportunity to show cause why the appeal should not be summarily dismissed` `the General Division considered my representations`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Supervision and revision of lower courts (ss 24 to 28)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 24. The General Division may call for and examine the record of any civil proceedings before any subordinate court, to satisfy itself as to the correctness, legality or propriety of a decision or the regularity of the proceedings.
@export Under section 24, may the General Division call for and examine the record of my civil proceedings in the lower court?
GIVEN `the lower court` IS A Court @desc Which court or tribunal are your civil proceedings in?
      `a written law gives a right of appeal from its decisions to the Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it is a body other than a court named in the Act: does a written law give a right of appeal from its decisions to the Supreme Court?
      `the purpose is to satisfy the General Division as to correctness, legality, propriety or regularity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the purpose to satisfy the General Division as to the correctness, legality or propriety of any decision, or the regularity of the proceedings?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 24, may the General Division call for the record of my civil proceedings` IS
    `s 24 the General Division may call for and examine the record of the civil proceedings` `the lower court` `a written law gives a right of appeal from its decisions to the Supreme Court` `a civil proceeding` `the purpose is to satisfy the General Division as to correctness, legality, propriety or regularity`

-- s 26. Where an appeal lies from any decision in any civil matter, and no appeal is brought, no proceeding by way of revision is to be entertained at the instance of a party who could have appealed.
@export Under section 26, can the General Division entertain my request for revision when I could have appealed but did not?
GIVEN `an appeal lies from the decision in my civil matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does an appeal lie from the decision in your civil matter?
      `no appeal is brought` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has no appeal been brought?
      `I am a party who could have appealed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a party who could have appealed against the decision?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 26, can a revision proceeding be entertained at my instance` IS
    `s 26 a proceeding by way of revision may be entertained at the instance of the party` `an appeal lies from the decision in my civil matter` `no appeal is brought` `I am a party who could have appealed`

-- s 27(2). The General Division may, if it appears desirable in the interests of justice, of its own motion or at the instance of any party or person interested, at any stage, call for the record of a matter or proceeding in a subordinate court, remove it into the General Division, or give directions on its further conduct.
@export Under section 27(2), may the General Division take this step in my matter in the lower court?
GIVEN `the step` IS A `s 27(2) step` @desc Which step: call for the record, remove the matter or proceeding into the General Division, or give the lower court directions as to its further conduct?
      `the lower court` IS A Court @desc Which court or tribunal is the matter in?
      `a written law gives a right of appeal from its decisions to the Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it is a body other than a court named in the Act: does a written law give a right of appeal from its decisions to the Supreme Court?
      `it appears desirable in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it appear desirable in the interests of justice to take the step?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 27(2), may the General Division take the step in my matter` IS
    `s 27(2) the General Division may take the step in the matter or proceeding` `the step` `the lower court` `a written law gives a right of appeal from its decisions to the Supreme Court` `it appears desirable in the interests of justice`

-- s 27(3). Upon the General Division calling for a record under s 27(2), all proceedings in the subordinate court in the matter are stayed pending further order of the General Division.
@export Under section 27(3), are the proceedings in the lower court stayed?
GIVEN `the General Division has called for the record under section 27(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the General Division called for the record under section 27(2)?
      `the General Division has made a further order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the General Division made a further order in the matter or proceeding?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 27(3), are the proceedings in the lower court stayed` IS
    `s 27(3) the proceedings in the subordinate court are stayed` `the General Division has called for the record under section 27(2)` `the General Division has made a further order`

-- s 28(1). Subject to any written law, no party has any right to be heard before the General Division when it exercises its powers of supervision and revision.
@export Under section 28(1), do I have a right to be heard when the General Division exercises its powers of supervision and revision?
GIVEN `the General Division is exercising its powers of supervision and revision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the General Division exercising its powers of supervision and revision?
      `a written law gives me a right to be heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law give you a right to be heard?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 28(1), do I have a right to be heard` IS
    `s 28(1) the party has a right to be heard before the General Division` `the General Division is exercising its powers of supervision and revision` `a written law gives me a right to be heard`

-- s 28(2). No final order may be made to the prejudice of any person unless that person has had an opportunity of being so heard.
@export Under section 28(2), may a final order be made to my prejudice without my having been heard?
GIVEN `the order is a final order made to my prejudice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a final order made to your prejudice?
      `I have had an opportunity of being heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you had an opportunity of being heard?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 28(2), may a final order be made to my prejudice` IS
    `s 28(2) the final order may be made to the prejudice of the person` `the order is a final order made to my prejudice` `I have had an opportunity of being heard`

-- s 28(2), the same sentence asked as the bar: the bar applies if the order is a final order made to my prejudice and I have not been heard; it does not once I have been heard.
@export Under section 28(2), does the bar on a final order made to my prejudice without my having been heard apply to me?
GIVEN `the order is a final order made to my prejudice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a final order made to your prejudice?
      `I have had an opportunity of being heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you had an opportunity of being heard?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 28(2), does the bar on a final order to my prejudice apply` IS
    `s 28(2) the bar on a final order made to the prejudice of the person applies` `the order is a final order made to my prejudice` `I have had an opportunity of being heard`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Appealing a decision of the General Division (ss 29 to 29E)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 29. In the following cases an appeal CANNOT be brought against a decision of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction: (a) a written law expressly provides the decision is final or that no appeal can be brought;
-- (b) a case specified in the Fourth Schedule, subject to any exception in it. JUST TRUE means that an appeal cannot be brought.
@export Under section 29, is this a case in which an appeal CANNOT be brought against the General Division's decision?
GIVEN `the court that made my decision` IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision you want to appeal: the General Division, the Singapore International Commercial Court, or another?
      `the jurisdiction the court exercised` IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc Did the court make the decision in its original jurisdiction, its appellate jurisdiction, or its supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction?
      `a written law says my decision is final or that no appeal lies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law expressly provide that the decision of the General Division is final or that an appeal cannot be brought against it?
      `the Fourth Schedule says no appeal can be brought` IS A `scja1 seat answer` @desc Is your case one specified in the Fourth Schedule, and not within any exception in it, so that no appeal can be brought?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 29, can no appeal be brought against the decision` IS
    `s 29 an appeal cannot be brought against the decision`
        (`scja1 seat my decision from what I know` `the court that made my decision` `the jurisdiction the court exercised` FALSE 0 `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` FALSE `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` `a written law says my decision is final or that no appeal lies`)
        (`scja1 seat what I say, as a fact` `the Fourth Schedule says no appeal can be brought`)

-- s 29A(1). In the following cases permission is required before an appeal may be brought against a decision of the General Division: (a) a written law expressly provides an appeal only with permission; (b) the amount in dispute or value
-- (excluding interest and costs) does not exceed $250,000 (or the sum an order under s 29A(3) specifies), subject to any exception in the Fifth Schedule; (c) a case in paragraph 3, 4(1) or 5(1) of the Fifth Schedule, subject to its exceptions.
@export Under section 29A(1), do I need permission before I can appeal against this decision of the General Division?
@desc The Act says permission is required in the cases it lists: a written law that expressly requires it, an amount in dispute or value of the subject matter that does not exceed $250,000 (or the sum an order sets) subject to the Fifth Schedule's exceptions, and the cases in paragraphs 3, 4(1) and 5(1) of the Fifth Schedule. JUST TRUE means you are in one of them. NOT COVERED does not mean you need no permission: this section does not say that you need permission in any other case (for example a claim of $300,000), so look at the Schedules and any other provision.
GIVEN `the court that made my decision` IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision you want to appeal: the General Division, the Singapore International Commercial Court, or another?
      `the jurisdiction the court exercised` IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc Did the court make the decision in its original jurisdiction, its appellate jurisdiction, or its supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction?
      `the amount in dispute, in dollars, or 0 if I do not know` IS A NUMBER @desc What was the amount in dispute, or the value of the subject matter, at the hearing before the General Division, excluding interest and costs, in dollars? (0 if you do not know)
      `a written law expressly provides permission is needed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law expressly provide that an appeal may be brought only with permission, or that no appeal may be brought except with permission?
      `an order has changed the $250,000 figure to this amount, in dollars, or 0 if none` IS A NUMBER @desc If the President has by order specified another sum in place of $250,000, what is it in dollars? (0 if no order has done so)
      `an exception in the Fifth Schedule takes my case out of the $250,000 rule` IS A `scja1 seat answer` @desc Does an exception specified in the Fifth Schedule take your case out of the amount-in-dispute rule?
      `my case is one in paragraph 3, 4(1) or 5(1) of the Fifth Schedule with no exception` IS A `scja1 seat answer` @desc Is your case one specified in paragraph 3, 4(1) or 5(1) of the Fifth Schedule, and not within any exception in that Schedule?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 29A(1), do I need permission before I can appeal` IS
    `s 29A(1) permission is required before an appeal may be brought against the decision`
        (`scja1 seat my decision from what I know` `the court that made my decision` `the jurisdiction the court exercised` FALSE `the amount in dispute, in dollars, or 0 if I do not know` `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` FALSE `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` FALSE)
        `a written law expressly provides permission is needed`
        (`scja1 seat my amount if I know it` `an order has changed the $250,000 figure to this amount, in dollars, or 0 if none`)
        (`scja1 seat what I say, as a fact` `an exception in the Fifth Schedule takes my case out of the $250,000 rule`)
        (`scja1 seat what I say, as a fact` `my case is one in paragraph 3, 4(1) or 5(1) of the Fifth Schedule with no exception`)

-- s 29A(2). Permission must be obtained from the court a written law specifies for the purpose (or the court which may grant permission), and in any other case from the court the Fifth Schedule specifies.
@export Under section 29A(2), is this the court from which I must obtain permission to appeal?
GIVEN `the court I am asking about` IS A Court @desc From which court are you asking whether you must obtain permission?
      `a written law specifies a court for permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law specify the court from which permission must be obtained, or the court which may grant permission?
      `the court that written law specifies` IS A Court @desc If a written law specifies a court: which one?
      `the Fifth Schedule specifies a court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Fifth Schedule specify the court from which permission must be obtained?
      `the court the Fifth Schedule specifies` IS A Court @desc If the Fifth Schedule specifies a court: which one?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 29A(2), must I obtain permission from this court` IS
    `s 29A(2) permission must be obtained from this court` `the court I am asking about`
        (IF `a written law specifies a court for permission` THEN JUST `the court that written law specifies` ELSE NOTHING)
        (IF `the Fifth Schedule specifies a court` THEN JUST `the court the Fifth Schedule specifies` ELSE NOTHING)

-- s 29B(2). Before any notice of appeal is filed, the Judge who made a decision (not after a trial) may hear further arguments if a party, or the Judge, requests before the earlier of (a) the time the judgment or order is extracted and
-- (b) the 15th day after the date of the decision. The 15th day is counted with the Interpretation Act (fork F12): the day of the decision is not counted, and a Sunday or public holiday moves it to the next day that is not one.
@export Under section 29B(2), is my request for further arguments in time?
GIVEN `the court that made my decision` IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision: the General Division, the Singapore International Commercial Court, or another?
      `the jurisdiction the court exercised` IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc Did the court make the decision in its original jurisdiction, its appellate jurisdiction, or its supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction?
      `my decision was made after a trial of an action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the decision made after the trial of an action?
      `the decision was made by a Judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the decision made by a Judge (rather than the Registrar)?
      `the date my decision was made` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the decision made?
      `the judgment or order has been extracted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the judgment or order relating to the decision been extracted?
      `the date the judgment or order was extracted` IS A DATE @desc If it has been extracted: on what date?
      `a notice of appeal has already been filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a notice of appeal already been filed against the decision?
      `a party or the Judge requests further arguments` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a party to the hearing, or the Judge, requested further arguments?
      `the date of the request` IS A DATE @desc On what date was (or will be) the request made?
      `there is a public holiday in the days after the decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there a public holiday in the days after the decision? (The 15th day is counted with the Interpretation Act, which moves a last day that falls on a Sunday or public holiday to the next day that is not one. This question allows for one public holiday.)
      `the date of that public holiday` IS A DATE @desc If so: on what date is that public holiday?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 29B(2), is my request for further arguments in time` IS
    `s 29B(2) the Judge who made the decision may hear further arguments in respect of the decision`
        (`scja1 seat my decision from what I know` `the court that made my decision` `the jurisdiction the court exercised` `my decision was made after a trial of an action` 0 `the date my decision was made` `the judgment or order has been extracted` `the date the judgment or order was extracted` FALSE)
        `the decision was made by a Judge` `a notice of appeal has already been filed` `a party or the Judge requests further arguments` `the date of the request`
        (IF `there is a public holiday in the days after the decision` THEN LIST `the date of that public holiday` ELSE EMPTY)

-- s 29B(4)(a). If a request has been made, a notice of appeal may not be filed until the Judge affirms, varies or sets aside the decision, or certifies that no further arguments are required. JUST TRUE means you may not yet file.
@export Under section 29B(4)(a), am I barred for now from filing my notice of appeal?
GIVEN `a request for further arguments has been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a request for further arguments been made under section 29B(2)?
      `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments?
      `the Judge has certified or is deemed to have certified that no further arguments are required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Judge certified, or is the Judge deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 29B(4)(a), am I barred for now from filing my notice of appeal` IS
    `s 29B(4)(a) a notice of appeal may not be filed against the decision yet` `a request for further arguments has been made` `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision` `the Judge has certified or is deemed to have certified that no further arguments are required`

-- s 29B(4)(b). If a request for further arguments has been made, the time for filing a notice of appeal begins on the date the Judge affirms, varies or sets aside the decision after hearing further arguments, or certifies (or is deemed to have certified) that no further arguments are required.
@export Under section 29B(4)(b), on what date does the time for filing my notice of appeal begin?
GIVEN `a request for further arguments has been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a request for further arguments been made under section 29B(2)?
      `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments?
      `the date the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision` IS A DATE @desc If so: on what date?
      `the Judge has certified or is deemed to have certified that no further arguments are required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Judge certified, or is the Judge deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required?
      `the date the Judge certified` IS A DATE @desc If so: on what date?
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
DECIDE `Under section 29B(4)(b), on what date does the time for filing my notice of appeal begin` IS
    `s 29B(4)(b) the date on which the time for filing a notice of appeal begins` `a request for further arguments has been made`
        (`scja1 seat the date if it has happened` `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision` `the date the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision`)
        (`scja1 seat the date if it has happened` `the Judge has certified or is deemed to have certified that no further arguments are required` `the date the Judge certified`)

-- s 29C. An appeal against a decision of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction is made to the Appellate Division, unless the Sixth Schedule or another written law provides for the Court of Appeal.
@export Under section 29C, to which court is my appeal to be made?
GIVEN `the court that made my decision` IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision you want to appeal: the General Division, the Singapore International Commercial Court, or another?
      `the jurisdiction the court exercised` IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc Did the court make the decision in its original jurisdiction, its appellate jurisdiction, or its supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction?
      `the Sixth Schedule says the appeal is made to the Court of Appeal` IS A `scja1 seat answer` @desc Does the Sixth Schedule provide that an appeal in your case is made to the Court of Appeal?
      `another written law says the appeal is made to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law other than the Sixth Schedule provide that the appeal is made to the Court of Appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE Court
DECIDE `Under section 29C, to which court is my appeal to be made` IS
    `s 29C the court to which the appeal is to be made`
        (`scja1 seat my decision from what I know` `the court that made my decision` `the jurisdiction the court exercised` FALSE 0 `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` FALSE `scja1 seat a date the rule ignores` FALSE)
        (`scja1 seat what I say, as a fact` `the Sixth Schedule says the appeal is made to the Court of Appeal`)
        `another written law says the appeal is made to the Court of Appeal`

-- s 29D(1), (2). The Court of Appeal may transfer to itself an appeal against a decision of the General Division that has been made to the Appellate Division, on its own motion, on a reference by the Appellate Division, or on a party's application -- but an application may only be on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Appellate Division in accordance with s 29C, or on grounds the Rules prescribe.
@export Under section 29D, may the Court of Appeal transfer my appeal to itself on my application?
GIVEN `the appeal is against a decision of the General Division and has been made to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your appeal against a decision of the General Division, and has it been made to the Appellate Division?
      `my application is on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Appellate Division in accordance with section 29C` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Appellate Division in accordance with section 29C?
      `my application is on grounds prescribed by the Rules of Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application on grounds prescribed by the Rules of Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 29D, may the Court of Appeal transfer my appeal to itself on my application` IS
    IF `the appeal is against a decision of the General Division and has been made to the Appellate Division`
    THEN `s 29D(2) the power in section 29D(1) may be exercised in this way` `s 29D(2)(c) on an application to the Court of Appeal by a party to the appeal` `my application is on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Appellate Division in accordance with section 29C` `my application is on grounds prescribed by the Rules of Court`
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any appeal against any decision of the General Division that has been made to the Appellate Division"

-- s 29E(1), (2). The Court of Appeal may transfer to the Appellate Division an appeal against a decision of the General Division in a civil cause or matter that has been made to the Court of Appeal, on its own motion or on a party's application -- but an application may only be on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Court of Appeal in accordance with s 29C.
@export Under section 29E, may the Court of Appeal transfer my appeal to the Appellate Division on my application?
GIVEN `the appeal is against a decision of the General Division in a civil cause or matter and has been made to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your appeal against a decision of the General Division in a civil cause or matter, and has it been made to the Court of Appeal?
      `my application is on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Court of Appeal in accordance with section 29C` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Court of Appeal in accordance with section 29C?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 29E, may the Court of Appeal transfer my appeal to the Appellate Division on my application` IS
    IF `the appeal is against a decision of the General Division in a civil cause or matter and has been made to the Court of Appeal`
    THEN `s 29E(2) the power in section 29E(1) may be exercised in this way` `s 29E(2)(b) on an application by a party to the appeal` `my application is on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Court of Appeal in accordance with section 29C`
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any appeal against any decision made by the General Division in any civil cause or matter ... that has been made to the Court of Appeal"

-- ============================================================
-- scja2: this part's seat text, unchanged (merged by concatenation)
-- ============================================================
-- Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation in force from 1 July 2024 -- THE SEAT LAYER for sections 30 to 72.
--
-- SEAT: a person who wishes to start, defend or appeal a civil case in the High Court or the Court of Appeal.
-- This layer phrases the rules of the modules imported above as the questions that person would ask:
-- which court has jurisdiction over my matter, how many Judges may hear it and who may not sit, how a case is heard and decided,
-- when a case may be dismissed without a hearing, what orders and further evidence a court may allow, the time limit for asking the full panel to rehear,
-- whether a new trial may be ordered, whether my appeal stops enforcement, and whether I may appeal from the Appellate Division.
-- Nothing here is new law.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS.  Every question takes plain inputs: yes/no, a number, a date, a list of dates, or a choice from a list, one for each fact the person can state, each with an @desc worded as the person would be asked.
--   * A fact the person may not know, because it is the answer of a schedule or of another part of the Act, is asked as "yes / no / I am not sure".  "I am not sure" becomes NOTHING, and a rule that cannot answer without it answers NOTHING.
--   * The records the modules expect (a decision, a split of opinion) are built inside each question, in its input mapping, from the person's answers; the SAME module rule is then called.  No rule is restated here.
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act itself gives; NOTHING means the Act does not answer on these facts (NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING (scja2)").  Never read NOTHING as no.
--
-- There is no seat for an official.  The Act's provisions about the appointment of Judges and officers, the sittings the Chief Justice appoints, the capital-case procedure of Division 4 and the criminal jurisdiction (ss 30, 34(1)(a), 34(2), 48, 52(1)(a), 52(2), 60D to 60M, 61, 63, 66, 67, 69, 70, 71) are in layer 1 and the person meets them only through the questions below.
-- Names starting `scja2 seat:` are this layer's own wiring helpers (not questions); a helper type and its constructors carry the prefix so that no other part's seat can share their names.

DECLARE `scja2 seat: my answer` IS ONE OF
    `scja2 seat: yes`
    `scja2 seat: no`
    `scja2 seat: I am not sure`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Wiring: the person's answers become the modules' inputs.  Not questions.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- An answer of yes, no or "I am not sure" as a MAYBE BOOLEAN.
GIVEN answer IS A `scja2 seat: my answer`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`scja2 seat: what I say, as a fact` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN `scja2 seat: yes` THEN JUST TRUE -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja2 seat: no` THEN JUST FALSE -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja2 seat: I am not sure` THEN NOTHING -- input mapping

-- The decision the person is appealing.  Only three fields are asked, because only these three are read by the rules that take a decision (ss 35(2)(a) and 53(2)(a));
-- the rest of the record is filled with fixed placeholders that no rule called from here reads.
GIVEN court IS A Court
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised`
      nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding`
GIVETH A Decision
`scja2 seat: the decision I am appealing` MEANS
    Decision WITH
        `made by` IS court -- input mapping
        `in the exercise of` IS jurisdiction -- input mapping
        `nature of the proceeding` IS nature -- input mapping
        `subject of the case` IS `any other civil matter` -- placeholder, not read
        `the order` IS Order WITH about IS `permission to defend proceedings`, `way it went` IS `granted unconditionally` -- placeholder, not read
        `made after the trial of an action` IS FALSE -- placeholder, not read
        `made by consent of the parties` IS FALSE -- placeholder, not read
        `amount in dispute or value of the subject matter, excluding interest and costs` IS NOTHING -- placeholder, not read
        `date made` IS Date 1 1 2025 -- placeholder, not read
        `date the judgment or order was extracted` IS NOTHING -- placeholder, not read
        `a written law provides that it is final or that no appeal lies` IS FALSE -- placeholder, not read
        `the parties agreed in writing, signed by or for each, that it is final` IS FALSE -- placeholder, not read

-- A split of opinion from the numbers of Judges on each side.
GIVEN for IS A NUMBER
      against IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Split of opinion`
`scja2 seat: how the Judges divided` MEANS
    `Split of opinion` WITH
        `Judges for the claim, appeal or application` IS for -- input mapping
        `Judges against it` IS against -- input mapping

-- The smaller panel the Seventh Schedule allows for the person's case, as the person says.
GIVEN allowed IS A `scja2 seat: my answer`
      panel IS A `Smaller panel`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Smaller panel`
`scja2 seat: the smaller panel the Seventh Schedule allows, from what I say` MEANS
    CONSIDER `scja2 seat: what I say, as a fact` allowed
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST panel -- input mapping
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING -- input mapping
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- input mapping



-- Whether the person's matter is one of the matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division (s 35(2)).
GIVEN court IS A Court
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised`
      nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding`
      `my matter is an appeal against that decision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`scja2 seat: my matter is of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division` MEANS
    `s 35(2) the matter is one of the matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division`
        (`s 35(2)(a) the matter is an appeal against a decision of the General Division in a civil cause or matter` (`scja2 seat: the decision I am appealing` court jurisdiction nature) `my matter is an appeal against that decision`)
        (`s 35(2)(b) the matter is an appeal or other process that lies, or is transferred, to the Appellate Division` `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Appellate Division` `my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division`)
-- Whether the person's matter is one of the matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal (s 53(2)).
GIVEN court IS A Court
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised`
      nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding`
      `my matter is an appeal against that decision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my appeal was transferred to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my matter is an application made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a common question of law or fact arises in my application and a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `relief I claim may affect a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `relief I claim may affect the outcome of a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`scja2 seat: my matter is of the civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    `s 53(2) the matter is one of the matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal`
        (`s 53(2)(a) the matter is an appeal against a decision of the General Division in a civil cause or matter` (`scja2 seat: the decision I am appealing` court jurisdiction nature) `my matter is an appeal against that decision`)
        (`s 53(2)(b) the matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division` `my matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division`)
        (`s 53(2)(c) the matter is an appeal or other process that lies, or is transferred, to the Court of Appeal` `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Court of Appeal` `my appeal was transferred to the Court of Appeal`)
        (`s 53(2)(d) the matter is an application to which either or both of the criminal-link limbs apply` `my matter is an application made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal` (`s 53(2)(d)(i) the application raises a common question with a matter in the criminal jurisdiction` `a common question of law or fact arises in my application and a criminal matter`) (`s 53(2)(d)(ii)(A) the relief claimed may affect a matter in the criminal jurisdiction` `relief I claim may affect a criminal matter`) (`s 53(2)(d)(ii)(B) the relief claimed may affect the outcome of a matter in the criminal jurisdiction` `relief I claim may affect the outcome of a criminal matter`))

§ `Part 4 -- The Appellate Division: my questions`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Which court has jurisdiction over my matter? (ss 31 and 35)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 31(1) with s 35(2).  The Appellate Division has the civil jurisdiction mentioned in section 35 over the matters section 35(2) lists.
@export Under section 31(1), does the Appellate Division have the civil jurisdiction over my matter?
GIVEN court IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision you want to appeal?
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc Was that decision made in the court's original jurisdiction, its appellate jurisdiction, or its supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction?
      nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding` @desc Was the case a civil proceeding, a quasi-criminal proceeding or a criminal proceeding?
      `my matter is an appeal against that decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal against that decision?
      `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law provide that your appeal or process is to lie to the Appellate Division?
      `my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your appeal transferred to the Appellate Division under section 29E(1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 31(1), does the Appellate Division have the civil jurisdiction over my matter` IS
    `s 31(1) the Appellate Division has the civil jurisdiction mentioned in section 35 over the matter` (`scja2 seat: my matter is of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division` court jurisdiction nature `my matter is an appeal against that decision` `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Appellate Division` `my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division`)

-- s 31(2).  The Appellate Division has no criminal jurisdiction.
@export Under section 31(2), does the Appellate Division have criminal jurisdiction over my case?
GIVEN nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding` @desc Is your case a civil proceeding, a quasi-criminal proceeding or a criminal proceeding?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 31(2), does the Appellate Division have criminal jurisdiction over my case` IS
    `s 31(2) the Appellate Division has criminal jurisdiction over the proceeding` nature

-- s 35(2).  The matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division: an appeal against a decision of the General Division in a civil cause or matter, or an appeal or process that lies or is transferred to the Appellate Division.
@export Under section 35(2), is my matter one of the matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division?
GIVEN court IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision you want to appeal?
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc Was that decision made in the court's original jurisdiction, its appellate jurisdiction, or its supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction?
      nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding` @desc Was the case a civil proceeding, a quasi-criminal proceeding or a criminal proceeding?
      `my matter is an appeal against that decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal against that decision?
      `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law provide that your appeal or process is to lie to the Appellate Division?
      `my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your appeal transferred to the Appellate Division under section 29E(1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 35(2), is my matter one of the matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division` IS
    `scja2 seat: my matter is of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division` court jurisdiction nature `my matter is an appeal against that decision` `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Appellate Division` `my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division`

-- 31(3).  The Appellate Division has the jurisdiction or power of the court or tribunal the appeal was brought from.
@export Under section 31(3), does the Appellate Division have the jurisdiction or power of the court or tribunal my appeal was brought from?
GIVEN `my matter is an appeal, or a purpose relating to an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal, or a purpose relating to an appeal?
      `the power is one that the court or tribunal I am appealing from has` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the power one that the court or tribunal you are appealing from has?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 31(3), does the Appellate Division have the jurisdiction or power of the court or tribunal my appeal was brought from` IS
    `s 31(3) the Appellate Division has the jurisdiction or power of the court or tribunal from which the appeal was brought` `my matter is an appeal, or a purpose relating to an appeal` `the power is one that the court or tribunal I am appealing from has`

-- 31(4).  The Appellate Division may decide any question that needs to be decided to do justice in any case before it.
@export Under section 31(4), may the Appellate Division decide a question that needs to be decided to do justice in my case?
GIVEN `the question needs to be decided to do justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the question need to be decided to do justice in your case?
      `the question arises in my case before the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the question arise in a case before the Appellate Division?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 31(4), may the Appellate Division decide a question that needs to be decided to do justice in my case` IS
    `s 31(4) the Appellate Division may decide the question` `the question needs to be decided to do justice` `the question arises in my case before the Appellate Division`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `How many Judges hear my case, and who may not sit? (ss 32, 33, 36)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 32(1).  Three or any greater uneven number of Judges.
@export Under section 32(1), may my case in the Appellate Division be heard by this number of Judges?
GIVEN `the number of Judges` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges will hear your case in the Appellate Division?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 32(1), may my case in the Appellate Division be heard by this number of Judges` IS
    `s 32(1) the number of Judges is one that the subsection provides for` `the number of Judges`

-- s 32(2).  A Judge must not sit to hear or decide an appeal from that Judge's own decision, or a related matter.
@export Under section 32(2), may a Judge sit in the Appellate Division on my appeal from that Judge's own decision?
GIVEN `my matter is an appeal from a decision made by the Judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal from a decision made by that Judge?
      `my matter is related to such an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter related to an appeal from a decision made by that Judge?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 32(2), may a Judge sit in the Appellate Division on my appeal from that Judge's own decision` IS
    `s 32(2) the Judge may sit in the Appellate Division to hear or decide the matter` `my matter is an appeal from a decision made by the Judge` `my matter is related to such an appeal`

-- s 32(3).  The Appellate Division may summon assessors as section 10A allows.
@export Under section 32(3), may the Appellate Division summon assessors to sit with it on my case?
GIVEN `section 10A allows the court to summon assessors in my case` IS A `scja2 seat: my answer` @desc Does section 10A allow the court to summon assessors in your case?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 32(3), may the Appellate Division summon assessors to sit with it on my case` IS
    `s 32(3) the Appellate Division may summon assessors as section 10A allows` (`scja2 seat: what I say, as a fact` `section 10A allows the court to summon assessors in my case`)

-- s 33(1).  The decision goes the way of the majority of the Judges.
@export Under section 33(1), does the decision of the Appellate Division go the way of the majority of the Judges?
GIVEN `Judges for the appeal or application` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the Judges hearing your case are for the appeal or application?
      `Judges against it` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the Judges hearing your case are against it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 33(1), does the decision of the Appellate Division go the way of the majority of the Judges` IS
    `s 33(1) the decision goes the way of the opinion of the majority of the Judges hearing the case` (`scja2 seat: how the Judges divided` `Judges for the appeal or application` `Judges against it`)

-- s 33(2).  If the Judges are evenly divided, the appeal or application is dismissed.
@export Under section 33(2), is my appeal or application dismissed because the Judges are evenly divided?
GIVEN `Judges for the appeal or application` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the Judges hearing your case are for the appeal or application?
      `Judges against it` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the Judges hearing your case are against it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 33(2), is my appeal or application dismissed because the Judges are evenly divided` IS
    `s 33(2) the appeal or application is dismissed because the Judges hearing it are evenly divided` (`scja2 seat: how the Judges divided` `Judges for the appeal or application` `Judges against it`)

-- s 36(1).  Despite section 32(1), a case may, if the Seventh Schedule so provides, be heard by a smaller panel.
@export Under section 36(1), may my case in the Appellate Division be heard and decided by this number of Judges?
GIVEN `the number of Judges` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges will hear and decide your case?
      `the Seventh Schedule allows fewer than 3 Judges in my case` IS A `scja2 seat: my answer` @desc Does the Seventh Schedule allow fewer than 3 Judges to hear your kind of case? Answer "I am not sure" if you cannot tell; the Act then gives no answer.
      `the smaller panel it allows` IS A `Smaller panel` @desc If so, which smaller panel does it allow?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 36(1), may my case in the Appellate Division be heard and decided by this number of Judges` IS
    `s 36(1) the case may be heard and decided by that number of Judges` `the number of Judges` (`scja2 seat: what I say, as a fact` `the Seventh Schedule allows fewer than 3 Judges in my case`) (`scja2 seat: the smaller panel the Seventh Schedule allows, from what I say` `the Seventh Schedule allows fewer than 3 Judges in my case` `the smaller panel it allows`)

-- s 36(2).  A Judge who made a direction or order under section 40(1) must not sit on an application to discharge or vary it.
@export Under section 36(2), may the Judge who made a direction or order in my case sit on my application to discharge or vary it?
GIVEN `my application is to discharge or vary a direction or an order the Judge made under section 40(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application to discharge or vary a direction or order that the same Judge made under section 40(1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 36(2), may the Judge who made a direction or order in my case sit on my application to discharge or vary it` IS
    `s 36(2) the Judge may sit in the Appellate Division to hear or decide the application` `my application is to discharge or vary a direction or an order the Judge made under section 40(1)`

-- s 36(3)(a).  If Judges of a panel of 3 or more are unable to continue, the remaining Judges, 2 or more, may carry on if every party consents.
@export Under section 36(3)(a), must the Appellate Division carry on and decide my case after some of its Judges can no longer continue?
GIVEN `the number of Judges who started` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges made up the Appellate Division when your case began?
      `the number of those Judges who cannot continue` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those Judges are unable, for any reason, to continue?
      `every party consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does every party to the case consent to the remaining Judges continuing?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 36(3)(a), must the Appellate Division carry on and decide my case after some of its Judges can no longer continue` IS
    `s 36(3)(a) the Appellate Division must continue to hear and decide the case` `the number of Judges who started` `the number of those Judges who cannot continue` `every party consents`

-- s 36(3)(b).  Otherwise the case must be reheard.
@export Under section 36(3)(b), must my case be reheard after some of the Judges can no longer continue?
GIVEN `the number of Judges who started` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges made up the Appellate Division when your case began?
      `the number of those Judges who cannot continue` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those Judges are unable, for any reason, to continue?
      `every party consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does every party to the case consent to the remaining Judges continuing?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 36(3)(b), must my case be reheard after some of the Judges can no longer continue` IS
    `s 36(3)(b) the case must be reheard` `the number of Judges who started` `the number of those Judges who cannot continue` `every party consents`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `How is my case heard? Can it be dismissed without a hearing? (ss 37, 38, 39)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 37(1).  The Appellate Division may decide a matter without hearing oral arguments unless the Rules of Court prescribe them.
@export Under section 37(1), may the Appellate Division decide my matter without hearing oral arguments?
GIVEN `the Rules of Court prescribe oral arguments for my kind of matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the Rules of Court prescribe oral arguments for your kind of matter?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 37(1), may the Appellate Division decide my matter without hearing oral arguments` IS
    `s 37(1) the Appellate Division may decide the matter without hearing oral arguments` `the Rules of Court prescribe oral arguments for my kind of matter`

-- s 37(3).  The Appellate Division may direct that a matter be heard asynchronously, by exchange of written correspondence.
@export Under section 37(3), may the Appellate Division direct that my matter be heard by exchange of written correspondence?
GIVEN `my matter is one the Appellate Division may decide without hearing oral arguments` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter one that the Appellate Division may decide without hearing oral arguments?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 37(3), may the Appellate Division direct that my matter be heard by exchange of written correspondence` IS
    `s 37(3) the Appellate Division may direct that the matter be heard in an asynchronous manner by exchange of written correspondence` `my matter is one the Appellate Division may decide without hearing oral arguments`

-- s 38(1).  Summary dismissal on the court's own motion.
@export Under section 38(1), may the Appellate Division summarily dismiss my appeal or application on its own motion?
GIVEN `the court acts on its own motion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Appellate Division acting on its own motion?
      `the court is satisfied that it has no jurisdiction to hear and determine my appeal or application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Appellate Division satisfied that it does not have the jurisdiction to hear and determine your appeal or application?
      `every issue was already decided in an earlier matter and my appeal or application has no merit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was every issue in your appeal or application already decided in an earlier matter involving you, so that it has no merit?
      `the court is satisfied that the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court are met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Appellate Division satisfied that the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court for summary dismissal are met?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 38(1), may the Appellate Division summarily dismiss my appeal or application on its own motion` IS
    `s 38(1) the Appellate Division may summarily dismiss the appeal or application on its own motion` `the court acts on its own motion` `the court is satisfied that it has no jurisdiction to hear and determine my appeal or application` `every issue was already decided in an earlier matter and my appeal or application has no merit` `the court is satisfied that the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court are met`

-- s 38(2).  Before summarily dismissing, the court must give an opportunity to show cause and consider the representations.
@export Under section 38(2), has the Appellate Division done what it must before summarily dismissing my appeal or application?
GIVEN `I was given a reasonable opportunity to show cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you given a reasonable opportunity to show cause why your appeal or application should not be summarily dismissed?
      `the court considered my representations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the court consider the representations you made?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 38(2), has the Appellate Division done what it must before summarily dismissing my appeal or application` IS
    `s 38(2) the Appellate Division has met the requirements before summarily dismissing, as the section requires` `I was given a reasonable opportunity to show cause` `the court considered my representations`

-- s 39.  An application that may be made to the General Division or to the Appellate Division must first be made to the General Division.
@export Under section 39, must I first make my application to the General Division?
GIVEN `my application may be made either to the General Division or to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc May your application be made either to the General Division or to the Appellate Division?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 39, must I first make my application to the General Division` IS
    `s 39 the application must first be made to the General Division` `my application may be made either to the General Division or to the Appellate Division`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `What orders and further evidence may the court allow? (ss 40, 41, 42)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 40(1).  In a pending appeal or application the court may make a direction or order incidental to it, an interim order, or an order for security for costs.
@export Under section 40(1), may the Appellate Division make this kind of direction or order in my pending appeal or application?
GIVEN `my appeal or application is pending before the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your appeal or application pending before the Appellate Division?
      kind IS A `s 40(1) kind of direction or order` @desc What kind of direction or order is it: incidental to the pending matter, an interim order, an order for security for costs (or for dismissal for failing to give it), or another kind?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 40(1), may the Appellate Division make this kind of direction or order in my pending appeal or application` IS
    `s 40(1) the Appellate Division may make the direction or order in the pending matter` `my appeal or application is pending before the Appellate Division` kind

-- s 40(4)(b).  A direction or order made by a single Judge may be challenged by an application to vary or discharge only with permission.
@export Under section 40(4)(b), may I apply to vary or discharge a direction or order a single Judge made in my case?
GIVEN `the direction or order was made by a single Judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the direction or order made by a single Judge?
      `permission to apply has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the single Judge or another Judge given you permission to apply to vary or discharge it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 40(4)(b), may I apply to vary or discharge a direction or order a single Judge made in my case` IS
    `s 40(4)(b) an application to vary or discharge the direction or order may be made` `the direction or order was made by a single Judge` `permission to apply has been given`

-- s 40(4)(b).  The decision to give or refuse that permission is final.
@export Under section 40(4)(b), is a Judge's decision to give or refuse me permission to apply to vary or discharge an order final?
GIVEN `the decision is a decision to give or refuse permission to apply to vary or discharge the direction or order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the decision a Judge's decision to give or refuse permission to apply to vary or discharge the direction or order?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 40(4)(b), is a Judge's decision to give or refuse me permission to apply to vary or discharge an order final` IS
    `s 40(4)(b) the decision to give or refuse permission is final` `the decision is a decision to give or refuse permission to apply to vary or discharge the direction or order`

-- s 41(4).  Further evidence on an appeal only with the court's permission and on special grounds, except as subsection (5) provides.
@export Under section 41(4), may I give further evidence to the Appellate Division on my appeal?
GIVEN `subsection (5) applies to my evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your evidence relate to matters that occurred after the date of the decision you are appealing against?
      `the Appellate Division has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Appellate Division given you permission to give further evidence?
      `there are special grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are there special grounds for giving further evidence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 41(4), may I give further evidence to the Appellate Division on my appeal` IS
    `s 41(4) the further evidence may be given to the Appellate Division` `subsection (5) applies to my evidence` `the Appellate Division has given permission` `there are special grounds`

-- s 41(5).  Evidence of matters after the date of the decision appealed against may be given without permission.
@export Under section 41(5), may I give evidence of matters after the decision I am appealing without permission?
GIVEN `my evidence relates to matters occurring after the date of the decision appealed against` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your evidence relate to matters that occurred after the date of the decision you are appealing against?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 41(5), may I give evidence of matters after the decision I am appealing without permission` IS
    `s 41(5) the further evidence may be given to the Appellate Division without permission` `my evidence relates to matters occurring after the date of the decision appealed against`

-- s 41(8).  A party may request the full panel to rehear arguments on an application for permission to adduce further evidence decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges.
@export Under section 41(8), may I ask the full panel to rehear the arguments on my application to adduce further evidence?
GIVEN `my application was heard and decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your application for permission to adduce further evidence heard and decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 41(8), may I ask the full panel to rehear the arguments on my application to adduce further evidence` IS
    `s 41(8) a party may request the full panel to rehear arguments on the application for permission to adduce further evidence` `my application was heard and decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges`

-- s 41(9).  No such request where the Judges who decided are the same number as the Judges hearing the appeal, or where 3 or more decided.
@export Under section 41(9), am I barred from asking the full panel to rehear my application to adduce further evidence?
GIVEN `the number of Judges who first decided my application` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges first decided your application for permission to adduce further evidence?
      `the number of Judges hearing my appeal` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges are hearing your appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 41(9), am I barred from asking the full panel to rehear my application to adduce further evidence` IS
    `s 41(9) the bar on a request under subsection (8) applies` `the number of Judges who first decided my application` `the number of Judges hearing my appeal`

-- s 41(10).  The request must be made before the judgment or order is extracted and within 7 days after the date of the decision, counted under section 50(b) of the Interpretation Act 1965.
@export Under section 41(10), is my request for the full panel to rehear my application to adduce further evidence in time?
GIVEN `the date I make my request` IS A DATE @desc On what date do you make your request?
      `the judgment or order had already been extracted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had the judgment or order already been extracted when you made your request?
      `the date the decision on my application was made` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the decision on your application for permission to adduce further evidence made?
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which days in the period are public holidays in Singapore? (a list of dates)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 41(10), is my request for the full panel to rehear my application to adduce further evidence in time` IS
    `s 41(10) the request under subsection (8) is made in time` `the date I make my request` `the judgment or order had already been extracted` `the date the decision on my application was made` `the public holidays`

-- s 42.  The Appellate Division may make an order as to the costs of the appeal and of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against.
@export Under section 42, may the Appellate Division make an order as to the costs of my appeal?
GIVEN `the order is as to the costs of an appeal or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the order one as to the costs of an appeal, or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 42, may the Appellate Division make an order as to the costs of my appeal` IS
    `s 42 the Appellate Division may make the order as to costs` `the order is as to the costs of an appeal or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Can a new trial be ordered? Does my appeal stop enforcement? (ss 43, 44, 45)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 43(1).  The Appellate Division may order a new trial of a matter tried by the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction.
@export Under section 43(1), may the Appellate Division order a new trial of my matter?
GIVEN court IS A Court @desc Which court tried your matter?
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc In which jurisdiction did that court try it: original, appellate, or supervisory and revisionary?
      `my matter was tried in the exercise of civil jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your matter tried as a civil matter, as opposed to a criminal one?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 43(1), may the Appellate Division order a new trial of my matter` IS
    `s 43(1) the Appellate Division may order a new trial of the matter` court jurisdiction `my matter was tried in the exercise of civil jurisdiction`

-- s 43(2).  No new trial on the ground of an improper admission or rejection of evidence unless it caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice.
@export Under section 43(2), may a new trial be ordered because evidence was wrongly admitted or rejected?
GIVEN `the ground is an improper admission or rejection of evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your ground that evidence was improperly admitted or rejected?
      `it caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did that cause a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 43(2), may a new trial be ordered because evidence was wrongly admitted or rejected` IS
    `s 43(2) a new trial may be ordered on that ground` `the ground is an improper admission or rejection of evidence` `it caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice`

-- s 43(3)(a).  Where the wrong affects only part of the case or only some parties, final judgment may be given for the unaffected part.
@export Under section 43(3)(a), may the Appellate Division give final judgment for the part of my case that was not affected?
GIVEN `the wrong affects only part of the case or only some of the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the substantial wrong or miscarriage of justice affect only part of the case, or only one or some of the parties?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 43(3)(a), may the Appellate Division give final judgment for the part of my case that was not affected` IS
    `s 43(3)(a) the Appellate Division may give final judgment in respect of the unaffected part of the case or the unaffected parties` `the wrong affects only part of the case or only some of the parties`

-- s 44.  No reversal, substantial variation or new trial for an error that does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction.
@export Under section 44, may the Appellate Division reverse or substantially vary a decision, or order a new trial, because of an error that does not affect the merits?
GIVEN `I ask the court to reverse or vary the decision or order a new trial because of an error, defect or irregularity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you ask the Appellate Division to reverse or substantially vary the decision, or to order a new trial, on account of an error, defect or irregularity?
      `the error does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the error, defect or irregularity leave the merits of the decision and the jurisdiction of the court whose decision is appealed against unaffected?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 44, may the Appellate Division reverse or substantially vary a decision, or order a new trial, because of an error that does not affect the merits` IS
    `s 44 the Appellate Division may reverse or substantially vary the decision, or order a new trial, on account of the error` `I ask the court to reverse or vary the decision or order a new trial because of an error, defect or irregularity` `the error does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction`

-- s 45(1).  An appeal does not stop execution or enforcement unless the court orders a stay.
@export Under section 45(1), does my appeal stop the decision I am appealing from being enforced?
GIVEN `a court has ordered a stay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court appealed from, or the Appellate Division, ordered a stay?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 45(1), does my appeal stop the decision I am appealing from being enforced` IS
    `s 45(1) the appeal operates as a stay of execution or enforcement or of proceedings under the decision appealed against` `a court has ordered a stay`

-- s 45(2).  No intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated except as the court directs.
@export Under section 45(2), is an act or step taken in my case while the appeal is pending invalidated?
GIVEN `the court directs that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court directed that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 45(2), is an act or step taken in my case while the appeal is pending invalidated` IS
    `s 45(2) the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated` `the court directs that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `May I appeal from the Appellate Division? (ss 46 and 47)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 46.  No appeal against a decision of the Appellate Division in the cases specified in the Ninth Schedule.
@export Under section 46, can I appeal against the decision of the Appellate Division?
GIVEN `the decision is a case specified in the Ninth Schedule` IS A `scja2 seat: my answer` @desc Is the Appellate Division's decision a case specified in the Ninth Schedule?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 46, can I appeal against the decision of the Appellate Division` IS
    `s 46 an appeal can be brought against the decision of the Appellate Division` (`scja2 seat: what I say, as a fact` `the decision is a case specified in the Ninth Schedule`)

-- s 47(1).  An appeal to the Court of Appeal against a decision of the Appellate Division in its appellate civil jurisdiction needs the Court of Appeal's permission.
@export Under section 47(1), may I appeal to the Court of Appeal against the decision of the Appellate Division?
GIVEN jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc Was the Appellate Division's decision made in its original jurisdiction, its appellate jurisdiction, or its supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction?
      nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding` @desc Was the case a civil proceeding, a quasi-criminal proceeding or a criminal proceeding?
      `the Court of Appeal has given me permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court of Appeal given you permission to appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 47(1), may I appeal to the Court of Appeal against the decision of the Appellate Division` IS
    `s 47(1) an appeal against the decision may be brought` (`scja2 seat: the decision I am appealing` `Appellate Division` jurisdiction nature) `the Court of Appeal has given me permission`

-- s 47(2).  The Court of Appeal may grant that permission only if the appeal will raise a point of law of public importance.
@export Under section 47(2), may the Court of Appeal grant me permission to appeal from the Appellate Division?
GIVEN `my appeal will raise a point of law of public importance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will your appeal raise a point of law of public importance?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 47(2), may the Court of Appeal grant me permission to appeal from the Appellate Division` IS
    `s 47(2) the Court of Appeal may grant permission under subsection (1)` `my appeal will raise a point of law of public importance`

-- s 47(4)(b).  Permission may be granted even if the appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division, or the Court of Appeal declined to transfer it to itself.
@export Under section 47(4)(b), may I be given permission to appeal even though my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division?
GIVEN `my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your appeal transferred to the Appellate Division under section 29E(1)?
      `the Court of Appeal declined to transfer my appeal to itself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the Court of Appeal decline to transfer your appeal to itself under section 29D(1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 47(4)(b), may I be given permission to appeal even though my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division` IS
    `s 47(4)(b) permission may be granted under subsection (1) despite the history of the appeal` `my appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division` `the Court of Appeal declined to transfer my appeal to itself`

§ `Part 5 -- The Court of Appeal: my questions`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Which court has jurisdiction over my matter? (ss 49 and 53)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 49(1) with ss 53(2) and 60D.  The Court of Appeal has the civil jurisdiction mentioned in section 53, the criminal jurisdiction mentioned in section 60D, and the jurisdiction Division 4 gives it.
-- A civil litigant's matter is not one of the criminal matters of section 60D, so that limb is asked of the same list with "another kind of matter".
@export Under section 49(1), does the Court of Appeal have jurisdiction over my matter?
GIVEN court IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision you want to appeal?
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc Was that decision made in the court's original jurisdiction, its appellate jurisdiction, or its supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction?
      nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding` @desc Was the case a civil proceeding, a quasi-criminal proceeding or a criminal proceeding?
      `my matter is an appeal against that decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal against that decision?
      `my matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal from the Appellate Division?
      `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law provide that your appeal or process is to lie to the Court of Appeal?
      `my appeal was transferred to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your appeal transferred to the Court of Appeal under section 29D(1)?
      `my matter is an application made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an application made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal?
      `a common question of law or fact arises in my application and a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your matter is an application: does a common question of law or fact arise in it and in a criminal matter before the Court of Appeal?
      `relief I claim may affect a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your matter is an application: may any relief you claim affect a criminal matter before the Court of Appeal?
      `relief I claim may affect the outcome of a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your matter is an application: may any relief you claim affect the outcome of a criminal matter before the Court of Appeal?
      `Division 4 of Part 5 provides that my application or action is to be dealt with by the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does Division 4 of Part 5 (the post-appeal application in a capital case) provide that your application or action is to be dealt with by the Court of Appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 49(1), does the Court of Appeal have jurisdiction over my matter` IS
    `s 49(1) the Court of Appeal has jurisdiction over the matter`
        (`scja2 seat: my matter is of the civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal` court jurisdiction nature `my matter is an appeal against that decision` `my matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division` `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Court of Appeal` `my appeal was transferred to the Court of Appeal` `my matter is an application made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal` `a common question of law or fact arises in my application and a criminal matter` `relief I claim may affect a criminal matter` `relief I claim may affect the outcome of a criminal matter`)
        (`s 60D the matter is one of the matters of the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal` `s 60D another kind of matter`)
        `Division 4 of Part 5 provides that my application or action is to be dealt with by the Court of Appeal`

-- s 53(2).  The matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal.
@export Under section 53(2), is my matter one of the matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal?
GIVEN court IS A Court @desc Which court made the decision you want to appeal?
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc Was that decision made in the court's original jurisdiction, its appellate jurisdiction, or its supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction?
      nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding` @desc Was the case a civil proceeding, a quasi-criminal proceeding or a criminal proceeding?
      `my matter is an appeal against that decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal against that decision?
      `my matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal from the Appellate Division?
      `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law provide that your appeal or process is to lie to the Court of Appeal?
      `my appeal was transferred to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your appeal transferred to the Court of Appeal under section 29D(1)?
      `my matter is an application made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an application made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal?
      `a common question of law or fact arises in my application and a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your matter is an application: does a common question of law or fact arise in it and in a criminal matter before the Court of Appeal?
      `relief I claim may affect a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your matter is an application: may any relief you claim affect a criminal matter before the Court of Appeal?
      `relief I claim may affect the outcome of a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your matter is an application: may any relief you claim affect the outcome of a criminal matter before the Court of Appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 53(2), is my matter one of the matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal` IS
    `scja2 seat: my matter is of the civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal` court jurisdiction nature `my matter is an appeal against that decision` `my matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division` `a written law provides that my appeal is to lie to the Court of Appeal` `my appeal was transferred to the Court of Appeal` `my matter is an application made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal` `a common question of law or fact arises in my application and a criminal matter` `relief I claim may affect a criminal matter` `relief I claim may affect the outcome of a criminal matter`

-- 49(2).  The Court of Appeal has the jurisdiction or power of the court or tribunal the appeal was brought from.
@export Under section 49(2), does the Court of Appeal have the jurisdiction or power of the court or tribunal my appeal was brought from?
GIVEN `my matter is an appeal, or a purpose relating to an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal, or a purpose relating to an appeal?
      `the power is one that the court or tribunal I am appealing from has` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the power one that the court or tribunal you are appealing from has?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 49(2), does the Court of Appeal have the jurisdiction or power of the court or tribunal my appeal was brought from` IS
    `s 49(2) the Court of Appeal has the jurisdiction or power of the court or tribunal from which the appeal was brought` `my matter is an appeal, or a purpose relating to an appeal` `the power is one that the court or tribunal I am appealing from has`

-- 49(3).  The Court of Appeal may decide any question that needs to be decided to do justice in any case before it.
@export Under section 49(3), may the Court of Appeal decide a question that needs to be decided to do justice in my case?
GIVEN `the question needs to be decided to do justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the question need to be decided to do justice in your case?
      `the question arises in my case before the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the question arise in a case before the Court of Appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 49(3), may the Court of Appeal decide a question that needs to be decided to do justice in my case` IS
    `s 49(3) the Court of Appeal may decide the question` `the question needs to be decided to do justice` `the question arises in my case before the Court of Appeal`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `How many Judges hear my case, and who may not sit? (ss 50, 51, 54)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 50(1).  Three or any greater uneven number of Judges.
@export Under section 50(1), may my case in the Court of Appeal be heard by this number of Judges?
GIVEN `the number of Judges` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges will hear your case in the Court of Appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 50(1), may my case in the Court of Appeal be heard by this number of Judges` IS
    `s 50(1) the number of Judges is one that the subsection provides for` `the number of Judges`

-- s 50(2).  A Judge must not sit to hear or decide an appeal from that Judge's own decision, or a related matter.
@export Under section 50(2), may a Judge sit in the Court of Appeal on my appeal from that Judge's own decision?
GIVEN `my matter is an appeal from a decision made by the Judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter an appeal from a decision made by that Judge?
      `my matter is related to such an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter related to an appeal from a decision made by that Judge?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 50(2), may a Judge sit in the Court of Appeal on my appeal from that Judge's own decision` IS
    `s 50(2) the Judge may sit in the Court of Appeal to hear or decide the matter` `my matter is an appeal from a decision made by the Judge` `my matter is related to such an appeal`

-- s 50(3).  The Court of Appeal may summon assessors as section 10A allows.
@export Under section 50(3), may the Court of Appeal summon assessors to sit with it on my case?
GIVEN `section 10A allows the court to summon assessors in my case` IS A `scja2 seat: my answer` @desc Does section 10A allow the court to summon assessors in your case?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 50(3), may the Court of Appeal summon assessors to sit with it on my case` IS
    `s 50(3) the Court of Appeal may summon assessors as section 10A allows` (`scja2 seat: what I say, as a fact` `section 10A allows the court to summon assessors in my case`)

-- s 51(1).  The decision goes the way of the majority of the Judges.
@export Under section 51(1), does the decision of the Court of Appeal go the way of the majority of the Judges?
GIVEN `Judges for the appeal or application` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the Judges hearing your case are for the appeal or application?
      `Judges against it` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the Judges hearing your case are against it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 51(1), does the decision of the Court of Appeal go the way of the majority of the Judges` IS
    `s 51(1) the decision goes the way of the opinion of the majority of the Judges hearing the case` (`scja2 seat: how the Judges divided` `Judges for the appeal or application` `Judges against it`)

-- s 51(2).  If the Judges are evenly divided, the appeal or application is dismissed.
@export Under section 51(2), is my appeal or application dismissed because the Judges are evenly divided?
GIVEN `Judges for the appeal or application` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the Judges hearing your case are for the appeal or application?
      `Judges against it` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the Judges hearing your case are against it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 51(2), is my appeal or application dismissed because the Judges are evenly divided` IS
    `s 51(2) the appeal or application is dismissed because the Judges hearing it are evenly divided` (`scja2 seat: how the Judges divided` `Judges for the appeal or application` `Judges against it`)

-- s 54(1).  Despite section 50(1), a case may, if the Seventh Schedule so provides, be heard by a smaller panel.
@export Under section 54(1), may my case in the Court of Appeal be heard and decided by this number of Judges?
GIVEN `the number of Judges` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges will hear and decide your case?
      `the Seventh Schedule allows fewer than 3 Judges in my case` IS A `scja2 seat: my answer` @desc Does the Seventh Schedule allow fewer than 3 Judges to hear your kind of case? Answer "I am not sure" if you cannot tell; the Act then gives no answer.
      `the smaller panel it allows` IS A `Smaller panel` @desc If so, which smaller panel does it allow?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 54(1), may my case in the Court of Appeal be heard and decided by this number of Judges` IS
    `s 54(1) the case may be heard and decided by that number of Judges` `the number of Judges` (`scja2 seat: what I say, as a fact` `the Seventh Schedule allows fewer than 3 Judges in my case`) (`scja2 seat: the smaller panel the Seventh Schedule allows, from what I say` `the Seventh Schedule allows fewer than 3 Judges in my case` `the smaller panel it allows`)

-- s 54(2).  A Judge who made a direction or order under section 58(1) must not sit on an application to discharge or vary it.
@export Under section 54(2), may the Judge who made a direction or order in my case sit on my application to discharge or vary it?
GIVEN `my application is to discharge or vary a direction or an order the Judge made under section 58(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application to discharge or vary a direction or order that the same Judge made under section 58(1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 54(2), may the Judge who made a direction or order in my case sit on my application to discharge or vary it` IS
    `s 54(2) the Judge may sit in the Court of Appeal to hear or decide the application` `my application is to discharge or vary a direction or an order the Judge made under section 58(1)`

-- s 54(3)(a).  If Judges of a panel of 3 or more are unable to continue, the remaining Judges, 2 or more, may carry on if every party consents.
@export Under section 54(3)(a), must the Court of Appeal carry on and decide my case after some of its Judges can no longer continue?
GIVEN `the number of Judges who started` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges made up the Court of Appeal when your case began?
      `the number of those Judges who cannot continue` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those Judges are unable, for any reason, to continue?
      `every party consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does every party to the case consent to the remaining Judges continuing?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 54(3)(a), must the Court of Appeal carry on and decide my case after some of its Judges can no longer continue` IS
    `s 54(3)(a) the Court of Appeal must continue to hear and decide the case` `the number of Judges who started` `the number of those Judges who cannot continue` `every party consents`

-- s 54(3)(b).  Otherwise the case must be reheard.
@export Under section 54(3)(b), must my case be reheard after some of the Judges can no longer continue?
GIVEN `the number of Judges who started` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges made up the Court of Appeal when your case began?
      `the number of those Judges who cannot continue` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those Judges are unable, for any reason, to continue?
      `every party consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does every party to the case consent to the remaining Judges continuing?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 54(3)(b), must my case be reheard after some of the Judges can no longer continue` IS
    `s 54(3)(b) the case must be reheard` `the number of Judges who started` `the number of those Judges who cannot continue` `every party consents`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `How is my case heard? Can it be dismissed without a hearing? (ss 55, 56, 57)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 55(1).  The Court of Appeal may decide a matter without hearing oral arguments unless the Rules of Court prescribe them.
@export Under section 55(1), may the Court of Appeal decide my matter without hearing oral arguments?
GIVEN `the Rules of Court prescribe oral arguments for my kind of matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the Rules of Court prescribe oral arguments for your kind of matter?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 55(1), may the Court of Appeal decide my matter without hearing oral arguments` IS
    `s 55(1) the Court of Appeal may decide the matter without hearing oral arguments` `the Rules of Court prescribe oral arguments for my kind of matter`

-- s 55(3).  The Court of Appeal may direct that a matter be heard asynchronously, by exchange of written correspondence.
@export Under section 55(3), may the Court of Appeal direct that my matter be heard by exchange of written correspondence?
GIVEN `my matter is one the Court of Appeal may decide without hearing oral arguments` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your matter one that the Court of Appeal may decide without hearing oral arguments?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 55(3), may the Court of Appeal direct that my matter be heard by exchange of written correspondence` IS
    `s 55(3) the Court of Appeal may direct that the matter be heard in an asynchronous manner by exchange of written correspondence` `my matter is one the Court of Appeal may decide without hearing oral arguments`

-- s 56(1).  Summary dismissal on the court's own motion.
@export Under section 56(1), may the Court of Appeal summarily dismiss my appeal or application on its own motion?
GIVEN `the court acts on its own motion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Court of Appeal acting on its own motion?
      `the court is satisfied that it has no jurisdiction to hear and determine my appeal or application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Court of Appeal satisfied that it does not have the jurisdiction to hear and determine your appeal or application?
      `every issue was already decided in an earlier matter and my appeal or application has no merit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was every issue in your appeal or application already decided in an earlier matter involving you, so that it has no merit?
      `the court is satisfied that the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court are met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Court of Appeal satisfied that the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court for summary dismissal are met?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 56(1), may the Court of Appeal summarily dismiss my appeal or application on its own motion` IS
    `s 56(1) the Court of Appeal may summarily dismiss the appeal or application on its own motion` `the court acts on its own motion` `the court is satisfied that it has no jurisdiction to hear and determine my appeal or application` `every issue was already decided in an earlier matter and my appeal or application has no merit` `the court is satisfied that the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court are met`

-- s 56(2).  Before summarily dismissing, the court must give an opportunity to show cause and consider the representations.
@export Under section 56(2), has the Court of Appeal done what it must before summarily dismissing my appeal or application?
GIVEN `I was given a reasonable opportunity to show cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you given a reasonable opportunity to show cause why your appeal or application should not be summarily dismissed?
      `the court considered my representations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the court consider the representations you made?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 56(2), has the Court of Appeal done what it must before summarily dismissing my appeal or application` IS
    `s 56(2) the Court of Appeal has met the requirements before summarily dismissing, as the section requires` `I was given a reasonable opportunity to show cause` `the court considered my representations`

-- s 57.  An application that may be made to the Court of Appeal or to another court must first be made to the other court.
@export Under section 57, must I first make my application to the other court?
GIVEN `my application may be made either to the Court of Appeal or to another court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc May your application be made either to the Court of Appeal or to another court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 57, must I first make my application to the other court` IS
    `s 57 the application must first be made to the other court` `my application may be made either to the Court of Appeal or to another court`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `What orders and further evidence may the court allow? (ss 58, 59, 60)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 58(1).  In a pending appeal or application the court may make a direction or order incidental to it, an interim order, or an order for security for costs.
@export Under section 58(1), may the Court of Appeal make this kind of direction or order in my pending appeal or application?
GIVEN `my appeal or application is pending before the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your appeal or application pending before the Court of Appeal?
      kind IS A `s 58(1) kind of direction or order` @desc What kind of direction or order is it: incidental to the pending matter, an interim order, an order for security for costs (or for dismissal for failing to give it), or another kind?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 58(1), may the Court of Appeal make this kind of direction or order in my pending appeal or application` IS
    `s 58(1) the Court of Appeal may make the direction or order in the pending matter` `my appeal or application is pending before the Court of Appeal` kind

-- s 58(4)(b).  A direction or order made by a single Judge may be challenged by an application to vary or discharge only with permission.
@export Under section 58(4)(b), may I apply to vary or discharge a direction or order a single Judge made in my case?
GIVEN `the direction or order was made by a single Judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the direction or order made by a single Judge?
      `permission to apply has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the single Judge or another Judge given you permission to apply to vary or discharge it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 58(4)(b), may I apply to vary or discharge a direction or order a single Judge made in my case` IS
    `s 58(4)(b) an application to vary or discharge the direction or order may be made` `the direction or order was made by a single Judge` `permission to apply has been given`

-- s 58(4)(b).  The decision to give or refuse that permission is final.
@export Under section 58(4)(b), is a Judge's decision to give or refuse me permission to apply to vary or discharge an order final?
GIVEN `the decision is a decision to give or refuse permission to apply to vary or discharge the direction or order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the decision a Judge's decision to give or refuse permission to apply to vary or discharge the direction or order?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 58(4)(b), is a Judge's decision to give or refuse me permission to apply to vary or discharge an order final` IS
    `s 58(4)(b) the decision to give or refuse permission is final` `the decision is a decision to give or refuse permission to apply to vary or discharge the direction or order`

-- s 59(4).  Further evidence on an appeal only with the court's permission and on special grounds, except as subsection (5) provides.
@export Under section 59(4), may I give further evidence to the Court of Appeal on my appeal?
GIVEN `subsection (5) applies to my evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your evidence relate to matters that occurred after the date of the decision you are appealing against?
      `the Court of Appeal has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court of Appeal given you permission to give further evidence?
      `there are special grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are there special grounds for giving further evidence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 59(4), may I give further evidence to the Court of Appeal on my appeal` IS
    `s 59(4) the further evidence may be given to the Court of Appeal` `subsection (5) applies to my evidence` `the Court of Appeal has given permission` `there are special grounds`

-- s 59(5).  Evidence of matters after the date of the decision appealed against may be given without permission.
@export Under section 59(5), may I give evidence of matters after the decision I am appealing without permission?
GIVEN `my evidence relates to matters occurring after the date of the decision appealed against` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your evidence relate to matters that occurred after the date of the decision you are appealing against?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 59(5), may I give evidence of matters after the decision I am appealing without permission` IS
    `s 59(5) the further evidence may be given to the Court of Appeal without permission` `my evidence relates to matters occurring after the date of the decision appealed against`

-- s 59(8).  A party may request the full panel to rehear arguments on an application for permission to adduce further evidence decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges.
@export Under section 59(8), may I ask the full panel to rehear the arguments on my application to adduce further evidence?
GIVEN `my application was heard and decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your application for permission to adduce further evidence heard and decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 59(8), may I ask the full panel to rehear the arguments on my application to adduce further evidence` IS
    `s 59(8) a party may request the full panel to rehear arguments on the application for permission to adduce further evidence` `my application was heard and decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges`

-- s 59(9).  No such request where the Judges who decided are the same number as the Judges hearing the appeal, or where 3 or more decided.
@export Under section 59(9), am I barred from asking the full panel to rehear my application to adduce further evidence?
GIVEN `the number of Judges who first decided my application` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges first decided your application for permission to adduce further evidence?
      `the number of Judges hearing my appeal` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Judges are hearing your appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 59(9), am I barred from asking the full panel to rehear my application to adduce further evidence` IS
    `s 59(9) the bar on a request under subsection (8) applies` `the number of Judges who first decided my application` `the number of Judges hearing my appeal`

-- s 59(10).  The request must be made before the judgment or order is extracted and within 7 days after the date of the decision, counted under section 50(b) of the Interpretation Act 1965.
@export Under section 59(10), is my request for the full panel to rehear my application to adduce further evidence in time?
GIVEN `the date I make my request` IS A DATE @desc On what date do you make your request?
      `the judgment or order had already been extracted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had the judgment or order already been extracted when you made your request?
      `the date the decision on my application was made` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the decision on your application for permission to adduce further evidence made?
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which days in the period are public holidays in Singapore? (a list of dates)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 59(10), is my request for the full panel to rehear my application to adduce further evidence in time` IS
    `s 59(10) the request under subsection (8) is made in time` `the date I make my request` `the judgment or order had already been extracted` `the date the decision on my application was made` `the public holidays`

-- s 60.  The Court of Appeal may make an order as to the costs of the appeal and of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against.
@export Under section 60, may the Court of Appeal make an order as to the costs of my appeal?
GIVEN `the order is as to the costs of an appeal or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the order one as to the costs of an appeal, or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 60, may the Court of Appeal make an order as to the costs of my appeal` IS
    `s 60 the Court of Appeal may make the order as to costs` `the order is as to the costs of an appeal or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Can a new trial be ordered? Does my appeal stop enforcement? (ss 60A, 60B, 60C)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 60A(1).  The Court of Appeal may order a new trial of a matter tried by the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction, or by the Appellate Division.
@export Under section 60A(1), may the Court of Appeal order a new trial of my matter?
GIVEN court IS A Court @desc Which court tried your matter?
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised` @desc In which jurisdiction did that court try it: original, appellate, or supervisory and revisionary?
      `my matter was tried in the exercise of civil jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your matter tried as a civil matter, as opposed to a criminal one?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 60A(1), may the Court of Appeal order a new trial of my matter` IS
    `s 60A(1)(a) the Court of Appeal may order a new trial of a matter tried by the General Division` court jurisdiction `my matter was tried in the exercise of civil jurisdiction`

-- s 60A(2).  No new trial on the ground of an improper admission or rejection of evidence unless it caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice.
@export Under section 60A(2), may a new trial be ordered because evidence was wrongly admitted or rejected?
GIVEN `the ground is an improper admission or rejection of evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your ground that evidence was improperly admitted or rejected?
      `it caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did that cause a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 60A(2), may a new trial be ordered because evidence was wrongly admitted or rejected` IS
    `s 60A(2) a new trial may be ordered on that ground` `the ground is an improper admission or rejection of evidence` `it caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice`

-- s 60A(3)(a).  Where the wrong affects only part of the case or only some parties, final judgment may be given for the unaffected part.
@export Under section 60A(3)(a), may the Court of Appeal give final judgment for the part of my case that was not affected?
GIVEN `the wrong affects only part of the case or only some of the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the substantial wrong or miscarriage of justice affect only part of the case, or only one or some of the parties?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 60A(3)(a), may the Court of Appeal give final judgment for the part of my case that was not affected` IS
    `s 60A(3)(a) the Court of Appeal may give final judgment in respect of the unaffected part of the case or the unaffected parties` `the wrong affects only part of the case or only some of the parties`

-- s 60B.  No reversal, substantial variation or new trial for an error that does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction.
@export Under section 60B, may the Court of Appeal reverse or substantially vary a decision, or order a new trial, because of an error that does not affect the merits?
GIVEN `I ask the court to reverse or vary the decision or order a new trial because of an error, defect or irregularity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you ask the Court of Appeal to reverse or substantially vary the decision, or to order a new trial, on account of an error, defect or irregularity?
      `the error does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the error, defect or irregularity leave the merits of the decision and the jurisdiction of the court whose decision is appealed against unaffected?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 60B, may the Court of Appeal reverse or substantially vary a decision, or order a new trial, because of an error that does not affect the merits` IS
    `s 60B the Court of Appeal may reverse or substantially vary the decision, or order a new trial, on account of the error` `I ask the court to reverse or vary the decision or order a new trial because of an error, defect or irregularity` `the error does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction`

-- s 60C(1).  An appeal does not stop execution or enforcement unless the court orders a stay.
@export Under section 60C(1), does my appeal stop the decision I am appealing from being enforced?
GIVEN `a court has ordered a stay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court appealed from, or the Court of Appeal, ordered a stay?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 60C(1), does my appeal stop the decision I am appealing from being enforced` IS
    `s 60C(1) the appeal operates as a stay of execution or enforcement or of proceedings under the decision appealed against` `a court has ordered a stay`

-- s 60C(2).  No intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated except as the court directs.
@export Under section 60C(2), is an act or step taken in my case while the appeal is pending invalidated?
GIVEN `the court directs that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court directed that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 60C(2), is an act or step taken in my case while the appeal is pending invalidated` IS
    `s 60C(2) the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated` `the court directs that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§ `Part 6 -- Officers and offices: my questions`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§ `Who may exercise the Registrar's powers? (s 62)`

-- s 62(2).  The Deputy Registrar or an Assistant Registrar may exercise the Registrar's powers, subject to Rules of Court.
@export Under section 62(2), may the Deputy Registrar or an Assistant Registrar exercise a power of the Registrar in my case?
GIVEN officer IS A `Officer of the Supreme Court` @desc Which officer is acting: the Registrar, Deputy Registrar, Assistant Registrar or another officer?
      `Rules of Court provide otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the Rules of Court provide otherwise for this power?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 62(2), may the Deputy Registrar or an Assistant Registrar exercise a power of the Registrar in my case` IS
    `s 62(2) the officer may exercise or perform the powers and duties conferred and imposed on the Registrar` officer `Rules of Court provide otherwise`

§§ `What must the Sheriff do, and who acts for the Sheriff? (ss 64 and 65)`

-- s 64(1)(a).  The Sheriff or the Sheriff's officer must execute the writs and process of the Supreme Court given to the Sheriff by the Court for that purpose.
@export Under section 64(1)(a), must the Sheriff execute the writ or process the Supreme Court gave to the Sheriff in my case?
GIVEN `the process was given to the Sheriff by the Supreme Court for that purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the writ or process given to the Sheriff by the Supreme Court for that purpose?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 64(1)(a), must the Sheriff execute the writ or process the Supreme Court gave to the Sheriff in my case` IS
    `s 64(1)(a) the Sheriff or the Sheriff's officer must execute the process` `the process was given to the Sheriff by the Supreme Court for that purpose`

-- s 65.  Where the Sheriff is an interested party in a cause or matter, the court may appoint someone to act in the Sheriff's place.
@export Under section 65, may the court appoint someone to act in the Sheriff's place in my case?
GIVEN `the Sheriff is an interested party in my cause or matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Sheriff an interested party in your cause or matter?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 65, may the court appoint someone to act in the Sheriff's place in my case` IS
    `s 65 the court may appoint an officer or a person to act in the place of the Sheriff` `the Sheriff is an interested party in my cause or matter`

§§ `Who may take my oath or affidavit? (s 68)`

-- s 68(2).  A commissioner for oaths appointed by the Senate of the Singapore Academy of Law may do the listed things, subject to any limitation expressed in the appointment.
@export Under section 68(2), may a commissioner for oaths appointed by the Senate do this thing for me?
GIVEN thing IS A `s 68(2) thing a commissioner for oaths may do` @desc What do you need the commissioner for oaths to do: receive an acknowledgement, administer an oath (for bail, an affidavit, an answer or plea, a witness, an executor or administrator, or a person in a cause or matter), take a statutory declaration, or something else?
      `the appointment limits the commissioner so as to exclude this thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the commissioner's appointment express a limitation that excludes this thing?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 68(2), may a commissioner for oaths appointed by the Senate do this thing for me` IS
    `s 68(2) the commissioner for oaths appointed by the Senate may do the thing` thing `the appointment limits the commissioner so as to exclude this thing`

§§ `Is the Registry open? (s 72)`

-- s 72(1).  The Registry is to be open every day except Saturdays, Sundays and public holidays.
@export Under section 72(1), is the Registry of the Supreme Court open on this day?
GIVEN day IS A DATE @desc On what date do you want to go to the Registry?
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which days around then are public holidays in Singapore? (a list of dates)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 72(1), is the Registry of the Supreme Court open on this day` IS
    `s 72(1) the Registry of the Supreme Court is to be open on the day` day `the public holidays`

-- s 72(2).  On a Saturday, Sunday or public holiday the Registrar may lawfully sit or carry out the Registry's business if the Chief Justice has directed it or the Registrar thinks the business extremely urgent.
@export Under section 72(2), may the Registrar carry out the Registry's business for me on this Saturday, Sunday or public holiday?
GIVEN day IS A DATE @desc On what date do you need the Registrar to act?
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which days around then are public holidays in Singapore? (a list of dates)
      `the Chief Justice has directed the Registrar to act on that day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Chief Justice directed the Registrar to sit or carry out the business of the Registry on that day?
      `in the opinion of the Registrar, my business is extremely urgent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In the Registrar's opinion, is the business to be despatched extremely urgent?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 72(2), may the Registrar carry out the Registry's business for me on this Saturday, Sunday or public holiday` IS
    `s 72(2) the Registrar may lawfully sit or carry out the business of the Registry on the day` day `the public holidays` `the Chief Justice has directed the Registrar to act on that day` `in the opinion of the Registrar, my business is extremely urgent`

-- ============================================================
-- scja3: this part's seat text, unchanged (merged by concatenation)
-- ============================================================
-- Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation in force from 1 July 2024
-- (plain-text rendering, not the official text) -- THE SEAT LAYER of part scja3: Part 7 (ss 73 to 83) and the First to Tenth Schedules.
--
-- SEAT: a person who wishes to start, defend or appeal a civil case in the High Court or the Court of Appeal.  This layer phrases the rules already
-- encoded in the modules imported above as the questions that person would ask.  Nothing here is new law, and no rule is restated here.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS.  Every question takes plain inputs (yes/no, a number, a date, or a choice from a list), one for each fact the person can state, each with an @desc
-- worded as the person would be asked.  The records the modules expect are built inside each question from those answers, and the SAME module rule is then called.
--   * A court is chosen from a list (`scja3 Which court`).  A field of a decision that no rule of the question reads is given a fixed placeholder, with a comment.
--     The fields that a rule does read are asked: the court, the order and how it went, consent, a written agreement that the decision is final, the jurisdiction (Schedules 4, 5 and 6),
--     and whether the decision followed the trial of an action (Schedule 4).  One fact stays fixed because this seat serves a civil case: the nature of the proceeding is civil in the
--     questions of Schedules 3, 4, 5 and 9 (the Fourth Schedule's paragraph 2 and the Fifth Schedule's paragraph 4 read it; Schedule 6 asks it).
--   * The court of section 29C, which section 29C answers (scja1), is asked as a choice among the Appellate Division, the Court of Appeal and "I do not know";
--     "I do not know" becomes NOTHING, and a rule that needs it answers NOTHING.
--   * Wherever a fact is a judgment of the court ("the court is satisfied that ..."), the question asks whether the court is satisfied; the person states it.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act itself gives; NOTHING means the Act does not answer on these facts
-- (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").  Never read NOTHING as no.
-- A question about a protection that a person has (ss 79(1), (2), (4), (5)) is worded as a question about the protection: JUST TRUE means the person IS protected.
--
-- There is no seat for an official.  Provisions that address only an official (ss 75, 80(1) to (3), 81, 83, the Second Schedule's oaths, the Tenth Schedule)
-- are encoded in layer 1 for what they decide and have no question here, or, for the Tenth Schedule, a criminal jurisdiction, are not exported.

DECLARE `scja3 Which court` IS ONE OF
    `scja3 the General Division`
    `scja3 the Singapore International Commercial Court`
    `scja3 the Family Division of the High Court`
    `scja3 the Appellate Division`
    `scja3 the Court of Appeal`
    `scja3 a District Court`
    `scja3 a Magistrate's Court`
    `scja3 another subordinate court, tribunal or judicial or quasi-judicial body`

DECLARE `scja3 Which appeal court` IS ONE OF
    `scja3 the Appellate Division as the appeal court`
    `scja3 the Court of Appeal as the appeal court`
    `scja3 I do not know which appeal court`

DECLARE `scja3 My answer` IS ONE OF
    `scja3 Yes`
    `scja3 No`
    `scja3 I am not sure`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Wiring: the person's answers become the modules' inputs.  Not questions.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

GIVEN which IS A `scja3 Which court`
GIVETH A Court
`scja3 my court` MEANS
    CONSIDER which
    WHEN `scja3 the General Division` THEN `General Division` -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 the Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN `Singapore International Commercial Court` -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 the Family Division of the High Court` THEN `Family Division of the High Court` -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 the Appellate Division` THEN `Appellate Division` -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 the Court of Appeal` THEN `Court of Appeal` -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 a District Court` THEN `District Court` -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 a Magistrate's Court` THEN `Magistrate's Court` -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 another subordinate court, tribunal or judicial or quasi-judicial body` THEN `another subordinate court, tribunal or judicial or quasi-judicial body` -- input mapping

GIVEN which IS A `scja3 Which appeal court`
GIVETH A MAYBE Court
`scja3 the court I appeal to` MEANS
    CONSIDER which
    WHEN `scja3 the Appellate Division as the appeal court` THEN JUST `Appellate Division` -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 the Court of Appeal as the appeal court` THEN JUST `Court of Appeal` -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 I do not know which appeal court` THEN NOTHING -- input mapping

GIVEN answer IS A `scja3 My answer`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`scja3 what I say, as a fact` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN `scja3 Yes` THEN JUST TRUE -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 No` THEN JUST FALSE -- input mapping
    WHEN `scja3 I am not sure` THEN NOTHING -- input mapping

-- A decision from what the person can state.  The date the decision was made and the date it was extracted are read by no rule of this part's questions: fixed placeholders.
-- The amount in dispute is read by no rule of this part's questions (section 29A(1)(b) is asked as a separate answer): NOTHING.
GIVEN court IS A Court
      jurisdiction IS A `Jurisdiction exercised`
      nature IS A `Nature of the proceeding`
      subject IS A `Subject of the case`
      about IS A `Subject of an order or application`
      way IS A `Way an order went`
      consent IS A BOOLEAN
      afterTrial IS A BOOLEAN
      writtenLaw IS A BOOLEAN
      agreedFinal IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Decision
`scja3 my decision` MEANS
    Decision WITH
        `made by` IS court
        `in the exercise of` IS jurisdiction
        `nature of the proceeding` IS nature
        `subject of the case` IS subject
        `the order` IS (Order OF about, way)
        `made after the trial of an action` IS afterTrial
        `made by consent of the parties` IS consent
        `amount in dispute or value of the subject matter, excluding interest and costs` IS NOTHING -- placeholder: no rule of the seat's questions reads it
        `date made` IS Date 1 7 2024 -- placeholder: no rule of the seat's questions reads it
        `date the judgment or order was extracted` IS NOTHING -- placeholder: no rule of the seat's questions reads it
        `a written law provides that it is final or that no appeal lies` IS writtenLaw
        `the parties agreed in writing, signed by or for each, that it is final` IS agreedFinal

§ `The Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 (Part 7 and the Schedules) -- my questions`


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `The Registrar's list of touts (s 73)`
-- Situation: asked only of a person whose name is on, or is sought to be put on, the Registrar's list of touts. The form can ask "Has the Registrar proposed to put your name on a list of touts?" before showing this group.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- section 73(1).
@export Under section 73(1), can the Registrar put my name on the list of touts?
GIVEN `I am proved to the Registrar's satisfaction to act as a tout or unauthorised adviser` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has it been proved to the Registrar's satisfaction, by evidence of general repute or otherwise, that you act as a tout or unauthorised adviser to suitors or other persons having business with or before an officer of the Supreme Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73(1), can the Registrar put my name on the list of touts` IS
    `s 73(1) the Registrar may include the person in the list of touts` `I am proved to the Registrar's satisfaction to act as a tout or unauthorised adviser`

-- section 73(3).
@export Under section 73(3), must my name stay off the list of touts for now?
GIVEN `I have been heard against the inclusion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been heard against your name being included in the list?
      `I have had an opportunity of being heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you had an opportunity of being heard against your name being included in the list?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73(3), must my name stay off the list of touts for now` IS
    `s 73(3) the person's name cannot yet be included in the list` `I have been heard against the inclusion` `I have had an opportunity of being heard`

-- section 73(2).
@export Under section 73(2), can the Registrar bar me from the precincts of the Supreme Court?
GIVEN `my name is included in the list` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your name included in the Registrar's list of touts?
      `I am a party to or a witness in proceedings in the Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a party to, or a witness in, any proceedings in the Supreme Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73(2), can the Registrar bar me from the precincts of the Supreme Court` IS
    `s 73(2) the Registrar may exclude the person from the precincts of the Supreme Court` `my name is included in the list` `I am a party to or a witness in proceedings in the Supreme Court`

-- section 73(2).
@export Under section 73(2), must I be allowed to remain in the precincts of the Supreme Court for such time as is necessary?
GIVEN `my name is included in the list` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your name included in the Registrar's list of touts?
      `I am a party to or a witness in proceedings in the Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a party to, or a witness in, any proceedings in the Supreme Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73(2), must I be allowed to remain in the precincts of the Supreme Court for such time as is necessary` IS
    `s 73(2) the person is to be allowed to remain in the precincts for such time as is necessary` `my name is included in the list` `I am a party to or a witness in proceedings in the Supreme Court`

-- section 73(4).
@export Under section 73(4), can I appeal to a Judge sitting in chambers in the General Division?
GIVEN `the Registrar made an order including my name in the list` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the Registrar make an order to include your name in the list?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73(4), can I appeal to a Judge sitting in chambers in the General Division` IS
    `s 73(4) an appeal lies to a Judge sitting in chambers in the General Division` `the Registrar made an order including my name in the list`

-- section 73(5).
@export Under section 73(5), can I appeal further from the Judge's decision?
GIVEN `the decision is the decision of the Judge sitting in chambers on my appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the decision the one the Judge sitting in chambers in the General Division made on your appeal against being included in the list?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73(5), can I appeal further from the Judge's decision` IS
    `s 73(5) a further appeal lies from the decision` `the decision is the decision of the Judge sitting in chambers on my appeal`

-- section 73(7).
@export Under section 73(7), is my name treated as included in the list under this Act because it appears in another list of touts?
GIVEN `my name appears in the list of touts under section 39 of the Family Justice Act 2014` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your name appear in the list of touts under section 39 of the Family Justice Act 2014?
      `my name appears in the list of touts under section 62 of the State Courts Act 1970` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your name appear in the list of touts under section 62 of the State Courts Act 1970?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73(7), is my name treated as included in the list under this Act because it appears in another list of touts` IS
    `s 73(7) the person is deemed to be included in the list under this section` `my name appears in the list of touts under section 39 of the Family Justice Act 2014` `my name appears in the list of touts under section 62 of the State Courts Act 1970`


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Measures against unmeritorious or vexatious proceedings (ss 73A to 73D and 74)`
-- Situation: asked only of a party whom the court, or the Attorney-General or another party, asks to stay, restrain or limit. The form can ask "Has anyone asked the court to stop or restrain your proceedings?" before showing this group.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- section 73A(2).
@export Under section 73A(2), can the court stay the legal proceedings I am conducting?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `scja3 Which court` @desc In which court are the legal proceedings?
      `the legal proceedings are in that court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the legal proceedings in that court?
      `the court is satisfied that I am conducting the legal proceedings in a vexatious manner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that you are conducting the legal proceedings in a vexatious manner?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73A(2), can the court stay the legal proceedings I am conducting` IS
    `s 73A(2) the court may order the legal proceedings stayed on such terms as it considers appropriate` (`scja3 my court` `the court`) `the legal proceedings are in that court` `the court is satisfied that I am conducting the legal proceedings in a vexatious manner`

-- section 73A(3).
@export Under section 73A(3), can the court order that I file no further documents in the legal proceedings?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `scja3 Which court` @desc In which court are the legal proceedings?
      `the legal proceedings are in that court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the legal proceedings in that court?
      `the court is satisfied that my filing further documents would be vexatious or for an improper purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that your filing further documents in the legal proceedings would be vexatious or for an improper purpose?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73A(3), can the court order that I file no further documents in the legal proceedings` IS
    `s 73A(3) the court may order that no further documents be filed by the party in relation to the legal proceedings` (`scja3 my court` `the court`) `the legal proceedings are in that court` `the court is satisfied that my filing further documents would be vexatious or for an improper purpose`

-- section 73A(5).
@export Under section 73A(5), can the General Division stay the legal proceedings I am conducting in a subordinate court?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `scja3 Which court` @desc Which court is being asked to make the order?
      `the legal proceedings are in a subordinate court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the legal proceedings in a subordinate court?
      `the General Division is satisfied that I am conducting the legal proceedings in a vexatious manner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the General Division satisfied that you are conducting the legal proceedings in a vexatious manner?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73A(5), can the General Division stay the legal proceedings I am conducting in a subordinate court` IS
    `s 73A(5) the General Division may order the legal proceedings in a subordinate court stayed on such terms as it considers appropriate` (`scja3 my court` `the court`) `the legal proceedings are in a subordinate court` `the General Division is satisfied that I am conducting the legal proceedings in a vexatious manner`

-- section 73A(6).
@export Under section 73A(6), can the General Division order that I file no further documents in the legal proceedings in a subordinate court?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `scja3 Which court` @desc Which court is being asked to make the order?
      `the legal proceedings are in a subordinate court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the legal proceedings in a subordinate court?
      `the General Division is satisfied that my filing further documents would be vexatious or for an improper purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the General Division satisfied that your filing further documents would be vexatious or for an improper purpose?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73A(6), can the General Division order that I file no further documents in the legal proceedings in a subordinate court` IS
    `s 73A(6) the General Division may order that no further documents be filed by the party in relation to the legal proceedings in a subordinate court` (`scja3 my court` `the court`) `the legal proceedings are in a subordinate court` `the General Division is satisfied that my filing further documents would be vexatious or for an improper purpose`

-- section 73A(7).
@export Under section 73A(7), can the court make the order against me?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `scja3 Which court` @desc Which court is being asked to make the order?
      `the order is one mentioned in section 73A(1)(a), (b) or (c), (2) or (3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the order a limited, extended or general civil restraint order, a stay of the proceedings, or an order that no further documents be filed?
      `the court acts on its own motion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court acting on its own motion?
      `the Attorney-General applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Attorney-General applied for the order?
      `a party in the legal proceedings applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a party in the legal proceedings applied for the order?
      `I have been given an opportunity to be heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been given an opportunity to be heard?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 73A(7), can the court make the order against me` IS
    `s 73A(7) the court may make the order against the other party` (`scja3 my court` `the court`) `the order is one mentioned in section 73A(1)(a), (b) or (c), (2) or (3)` `the court acts on its own motion` `the Attorney-General applies` `a party in the legal proceedings applies` `I have been given an opportunity to be heard`

-- section 73A(8).
@export Under section 73A(8), can the General Division make the order against me in respect of legal proceedings in a subordinate court?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `scja3 Which court` @desc Which court is being asked to make the order?
      `the order is one mentioned in section 73A(4)(a), (b) or (c), (5) or (6)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the order a limited, extended or general civil restraint order, a stay of the proceedings, or an order that no further documents be filed, in respect of proceedings in a subordinate court?
      `the Attorney-General applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Attorney-General applied for the order?
      `a party in the legal proceedings in the subordinate court applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a party in the legal proceedings in the subordinate court applied for the order?
